CinefexSearchData.register("text/20",{"issue":20,"date":"January 1985","pages":[["1","Cover","number 20 $4.75 Canada - $5.95 cine ex"],["2","Closing pages",""],["3","Contents","cinefex CINEFEX 20 - January 1985. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516, Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $15; eight issues - $25; twelve issues - $35. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Sunnymead, California: color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright © 1985 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor Adam Eisenberg contributing editors Paul M. Sammon Jordan Fox circulation manager JoDyne Shay editorial services Ann Dredla ..the journal of cinematic illusions Jupiter Revisited - The Odyssey of '2010' FRONT COVER - The Starchild appears in the Discovery pod bay in 2010. 4 Sixteen years ago, 2001: A Space Odyssey was launched into the cinematic firmament - a glimmering enigma which, among other things, promptly established a new aesthetic, as well as a whole new set of ground rules for motion picture special effects. Nearly a decade would pass before Star Wars and Close Encounters of the Third Kind, using computerized motion control photography and advanced compositing techniques, would begin to close the technological gap established by 2001. When Arthur C. Clarke published his much anticipated sequel 2010: Odyssey Two in 1982, it was reasonable to assume that it would soon find its way onto the screen, with the full force of contemporary state-of-the-art movie magic brought to bear on its complex effects requirements. Rising to the challenge were producer-writer-director Peter Hyams, futurist designer Syd Mead and visual effects supervisor Richard Edlund. From actualizing a Russian interplanetary spaceship to devising an authentic-looking representation of Jupiter's turbulent surface, the 2010 effects unit fulfilled the demands of the production and in the process established a fresh new look in simulated space photography. Article by Adam Eisenberg and Don Shay. Jupiter collapses in on itself at the climax of 2010 - BACK COVER."],["4","2010","JUPITER REVISITED The Odyssey of '2010'"],["5","2010",""],["6","2010","When Stanley Kubrick's 2001: A Space Odyssey first came to the screen, the year was 1968 and the human race had yet to land on the moon. This fact alone certainly insured that 2001 would become one of the most talked about new films of that year since its creators had gone to great lengths to depict as realistically as possible what future space travel would someday be like. For the audiences of 1968, the film represented the world of tomorrow, a time of regular missions to the moon and voyages beyond Jupiter. Just fifteen short years later, however, when Peter Hyams agreed to direct Arthur C. Clarke's sequel, 2010, all of that had been changed forever. In fact, in the intervening years since Kubrick's original film had stirred the imaginations of millions, mankind had not only set foot on the moon, several times, but had also sent unmanned probes to explore Mars, Jupiter, Saturn and the outer edges of the solar system. And with the introduction of the space shuttle in the early 1980s, regular space travel had finally arrived. Suddenly, within a very short period of time, Kubrick's vision of the future had become part of the present, and the year 2001 did not seem so very far off after all. As a result, in tackling the film adaptation of 2010, Hyams and visual effects supervisor Richard Edlund (ASC) found themselves in the uncomfortable position of not only having to go beyond the present, but also the future as depicted in the original film. And in doing so, they had to compete with the realities of today - flyby images of Jupiter sent back from close range by Voyager, and the now commonplace phenomenon of real astronauts wrestling equipment and satellites high above the earth. As had been the case with 2001, the ultimate in state-of-the-art special effects technology would be brought to bear on the project; but even so, the filmmakers found themselves on a difficult odyssey of their own as they tried to create a new vision of the future in less than half the time it had taken Kubrick to make the original film. Completed in 1982, Clarke's novel returned to the next century some nine years after the original mission of the American spaceship Discovery. Eager to learn the secrets hidden in the abandoned craft, American and Russian scientists hesitantly agree to a joint mission on board the Russian ship Leonov. Led by Heywood Floyd (Roy Scheider), the American team and their Russian counterparts eventually reach the Jovian system and revive the Discovery and its dysfunctional HAL 9000 supercomputer. With the mysterious monolith hovering nearby, the scientists soon discover that one of the original Discovery crew members, Dave Bowman, is still alive - at least on some plane of existence - and that the alien force behind the monoliths has an extraordinary plan for reshaping the solar system and, ultimately, the destiny of mankind. Shortly after the book was completed, Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer - which had produced the original film - announced its decision to bring the sequel to the screen as well. Upon learning that Kubrick himself was not interested in becoming involved, studio chief Frank Yablans asked Peter Hyams to consider doing 6 • CINEFEX 20"],["7","2010","the film. But Hyams, who had considerable experience dealing with the climates of space - having previously directed Capricorn One and Outland - immediately rejected the notion. \"My reasons were obvious. I don't think anybody in his right mind would want to be compared to Stanley Kubrick, especially if they're like me and have the belief that Kubrick is the greatest director alive in the world. So I said, 'No way,' but Frank said, 'Well, why don't you read the book first before you say no. I thought that was a depressingly logical suggestion, so I read the book and found in it two major things that changed my mind. First, there was the chance to make a film that I thought would be so different from 2001 in every aspect that it would be almost impossible to compare it to the original. And second, to make a film on what I think is the most extraordinary notion in the world - that of making contact with an alien race. Also, I saw in the story the chance to make a film that ultimately was about peace. Most films dealing with the future, more often than not, tend to be aimed at your eyeballs; but with 2010, I saw the opportunity to make one that would hopefully not only be exciting to look at, but would also be aimed at your Though intrigued by the book, Hyams realized that the visual effects in 2010 would have to be top-notch and sufficiently different from any other space film made in order to achieve the level of realism he thought the subject matter dictated. As a result, even after Hyams agreed to consider the project, he reasoned that the ultimate success or failure of the film would rest, to a large degree, on how well those effects could be executed. \"I committed to the project with a caveat - and so did M-G-M. I said to Frank Yablans, 'We should first spend X-amount of money and X-amount of time seeing if the film is feasible, because if it's not going to look credible, then we shouldn't do it.' And he agreed.\" For Hyams, the whole concept of credibility was closely linked to his personal philosophy on special effects. \"My idea of what effects work in a film and what effects don't is quite different than some other people's. I wanted the effects in this film to look unlike anything anyone had seen - and I mean every shot. And I wanted the process to be part of my film - I didn't want it to be an adjunct to my film. I light differently than other people light. It's not the right way or the wrong way — it's just my way. And I think that every shot of a film has to have an emotion behind it. 2010 is not a fantasy. What we're dealing with here is something purporting to be reality. And we're dealing with a fascinating time period. The year 2010 sounds far away, but it's not. Every teenager seeing this movie will be a young adult in the year 2010. So we're really dealing with our time - not some future generation's time - and what I wanted to do in this film was make something that looked convincing, as opposed to something that just looked impressive.\" To determine the feasibility of the film, Hyams turned to Richard Edlund in the spring of 1983. At the time, the multiOscared effects supervisor was still at Industrial Light and Magic, though he had just agreed to take over exclusive operation of the Entertainment Effects Group facility established by Douglas Trumbull and Richard Yuricich. \"When I started talking with Peter,\" said Edlund, then in the process of formulating Boss Film Corporation as the production entity for EEG, \"it became obvious right away that he was after a different look. I also was interested in coming up with a new look for a space picture. The Star Wars films, for instance, were all fantasies with a stylistic integrity that did not demand a sense of total realism. 2010, on the other hand, did demand total realism, and it would have to reflect what audiences today have seen in real space missions. Also, Star Wars was really like the Battle of Britain in outer space. It involved very fast action in which any one particular shot was on the screen for no more than three seconds. The images in 2010, however, would have to be much slower and more graceful, and since any effect left on the screen long enough will show its seams, I knew that the quality of the shots in 2010 would have to be that much better.\" Lager to accept these challenges, Edlund convinced Hyams that, indeed, the future visions of 2010 could be realized. As a result, Hyams plunged into the writing of the screenplay. Because of the tight deadline imposed by the studio, all of the design work for the film had to evolve simultaneously with the writing, \"There was never preproduction,\" Hyams noted. \"The only way we could make a film like 2010 in sixteen or seventeen months was to literally not have a schedule of preproduction, production and postproduction. That was just out the window. I started to design and started to draw all of the things that had to be made and all of the systems that had to be built, as I was writing. We started the effects work the minute I was convinced that the film could be done.\" While adapting the novel for the screen, Hyams relied on a technique he had found helpful many times in the past - using drawings to help him visualize characters, settings and scenes as he wrote. Usually, Hyams would render his own illustrations, but on 2010, he had the additional expertise of EEG art director George Jenson at his disposal. \"The first thing I did with Peter was to start drawing thumbnail sketches,\" Jenson recalled, \"to come up with concepts and compositions that would help us set the style of what the space effects sequences were going to look like. He had a lot of ideas of what he wanted to see. One strong composition that he had in mind from the very beginning was the shot that you see at the start of the film with Jupiter hanging down in the frame - this big ominous shape. It was a pretty strong image because of the psychological impact of seeing something large like that and looking up at it rather than looking down on it or from the side. So that first composition began to set the tone for a lot of the others. What I tried to do in designing this film was to keep to very classical, clean compositions and designs. Since we were dealing with geometric shapes - planets and things - we thought the best way to design the sequences was to keep them very pure in terms of the approach. For the most part, we used little sketches and article by Adam Eisenberg & Don Shay"],["8","2010","Science fiction master Arthur director Peter Hyams during effects supervisor Richard Entertainment Effects Group photography for the longdesigned the Leonov exterior vessel's interior sets. / In an craft. / The initial phase of entailed the construction of a three-foot-long foam core being gauged against a likeC. Clarke confers with a visit to the 2010 set. / Four-time Oscar-winning pioneered a new look in awaited sequel to 2001. / Visual futurist Syd Mead and provided specific detailing concepts for the early conference at EEG, Hyams studies Mead's drawings for the Russian the model-building effort mockup of the Leonov - scaled mockup of the Discovery by production designer Albert Brenner. Edlund and his simulated space 8 • CINEFEX 20 diagrams, but when it came to the Europa change and things that involved color and very deliberate, very conscious compositions, then I would do little paintings and we would discuss color and mood and what we were trying to say emotionally with those sequences.\" Jenson would work out the various ideas in rough sketches and then, when they were approved, Brent Boates would execute a finished storyboard. Sometimes, however, reaching that point of acceptance was difficult because of the problems inherent in conceptualizing many of the sequences. \"We had to approach things very carefully because of the lighting throughout the show,\" Jenson continued. \"In the Discovery boarding sequence, for instance, the Discovery is turning, and it was incredibly complicated because we had a constant light source on it as it was turning and our men were traversing and the light was constantly changing on them. So through that whole sequence, we had to be very careful of where our light was and, as a result, where our shadows were. That sort of complexity to the shots was true throughout the show. Every time we showed a planet or moon with a terminator, we had to be certain that it was consistent with the way our ships were lit and the way our people were lit. So it was very involved in that way. I think the Discovery boarding sequence was probably the toughest thing I've ever worked on, for that reason.\" Despite such difficulties, once the boards were approved, most did not require further alterations. \"Occasionally, though. there were cases where we would run into one or two drawings that needed to be changed - some for technical reasons. For instance, all the sequences where we had the guys on wires were easy to draw, but when I went over to the soundstage and saw the limitations of the rigging to actually fly those guys, a lot of the concepts had to be changed because of the pure physical problems of the rigging and how it worked against the sets. So there had to be some very fast compositional changes there, both to keep from seeing the wires and to accommodate the fact that they could only move in certain ways. The wires were hooked onto the actors' hips and they could go in certain directions between two parallel tracks, so there could be no turns in space and things like that.\" All of the storyboards were designed to take into account not only the realities of film production, but also the realities of space. \"Space is so vast and essentially black,\" noted Edlund, \"that it doesn't offer a lot of compositional possiblities unless you create them - unless you set up the scenes from the standpoint of composition before you shoot. So the whole picture was designed very carefully before we could start shooting any of the models. For instance, the geography of space was designed into all the boards from the start. We had a geography of the situations in mind for the entire picture, enabling us to know where we were in relation to the sun, Jupiter and her moons. We always needed to know where our light sources would be coming from so we could make certain that the shots were composited correctly and realistically. I"],["9","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 9"],["10","2010","10 CINEFEX 20"],["11","2010","think it was successful, since you always seem to know where you are when cutting outside the ship.\" As Hyams worked to complete his screenplay and Jenson labored over storyboard compositions, another aspect of 2010's conceptualization was also underway - the designing of the Russian spaceship Leonov and its attendant hardware. Acting on the recommendation of Richard Edlund, Peter Hyams engaged Syd Mead for the task. \"I doubt if there was anybody much more important to the project than Syd in terms of the design concept for the film,\" said Hyams, \"which from beginning to end was conceived to be functional. As I had in Outland, I wanted things to look like they really did something. Syd is really amazing at being able to extrapolate real surface detail and make it look convincing. He is not one of those people who just paints wonderfully imaginative paintings - he is an industrial designer by trade, and the bulk of what he does is real.\" Mead, whose expertise had already been tapped to make the speculative futures in Star Trek - The Motion Picture, Tron and Blade Runner seem like reality, approached the task of creating the Leonou with customary thoroughness and enthusiasm. \"During our first meeting, Peter described to me very exactly what he wanted the spaceship to do. He wanted it to look more like a complex than the Discovery - a working complex or habitat with a rocket motor attached to get it to Jupiter. So I knew we weren't into sleek shapes and nicely encased tubing. We were into a pretty utilitarian-looking complex which, for reasons of organization, I arranged along a long axis. The other parameter he stressed, along with the fact that he wanted the overall length to be about three hundred feet, was that in building the interior sets for the ship, he wanted to do away with weightlessness so that he could have dramatic action going on inside the ship without necessarily having to duplicate the floating effect of 'zero-G. So, to fulfill this requirement, we decided to rotate the cabin block.\" In addition to parameters dictated by aesthetics and practicality, Mead had also to take into account the fact that the Leonov was supposedly built by the Russian government, and therefore needed to reflect the sensitivities of its designers. \"To give it that strange 'Russian look,' I knew it had to be a little bit awkward, so my original intention was to have a spur come off the main axis and that would be the bridge. Then your docking port would be in line so that, in effect, you could be in the bridge and look out the window and monitor the docking process at the port end. But Peter, after seeing that, decided that he wanted it all on one axis so that it would make a statement immediately and you could do sort of an in-head establishing shot. From there, it became a matter of inventing the detail.\" Creating that detail - Russian or otherwise - and making it look believably futuristic was no easy task, not only because of the abundance of space films made since the original 2001, but also the large number of real space missions that have gone up since then. \"It is harder today,\" Mead explained, \"because the media just loads people with specific detail. We have color TV Modelmakers Nicholas Seldon and Ken Swenson confer during the final airbrush painting of the fully articulated, twelve-and-ahalf-foot Leonov miniature. / Mark Stetson's modelshop crew adds detailing to the nearly completed spacecraft. / Airbrush artist Ron Gress garnishes the communications module with further detail. / Seldon and Leslie Ekker work on last-minute detailing and wiring. / Miniature mechanical effects technician Bob Johnston examines the rotating crew quarters section on the smaller six-foot version of the Leonov, constructed in scale to the twelve-and-ahalf-foot Discovery used during most of the film. CINEFEX 20 • 11 ЛЕОН CR"],["12","2010","Assembled in the ward room of the Leonov, American and readings on the Jovian moon windowless, except in the bridge, the spacecraft's only eyes to the outside world are strategically throughout the ship. / The task of actually constructing the complex sets - a four-month project involving nearly sixty people served as his own director of levels and practical lighting Russian crew members discuss how best to deal with the unexpected discovery of lifeform of Europa. Totally its clusters of video monitors scattered - fell to special effects supervisor Henry Millar. / Peter Hyams, who also photography, employed predominantly low light to illuminate his sets. 12 • CINEFEX 20 from the shuttle - inside the working environment. People know what space hardware looks like, and they've now seen shots of the mockups of the space station - what we expect it to do. As a result, the public has a vision of what it should look like, right now; and if you go too far past that, they probably won't believe it.\" Besides the Leonou, Mead also had to design the unmanned probe that the scientists send to investigate the moon of Europa. \"I started with a very sleek, machined, tubular-like construction that would open up and become this ungainly system of antennae, TV cameras and landing lights. Peter then had me go back over the design and make it look even more awkward. It would still have to fold up so it could be packed on board the ship, but ultimately it looked less sleek and more squatty. \"Then, since Peter was writing the story all during this time, the next thing that was needed was some way to get from the Leonou to the Discovery to transfer materials or personnel back and forth. To solve this, I came up with a system of frames that when stored would nest together inside a triangular well in the side of the Leonov. Then, when the frames are stretched out, they bridge across and lock onto the door of the Discovery with magnets. We came up with these big clunky electromagnets because we figured that in outer space you could use superconduction technology with fairly low battery current. Then we produced a little trolley that traveled across the bridge on Even though the script was still not completed, the model work had to begin immediately to accommodate the tight production schedule. Once the designs for the Leonov and its various accouterments were finalized, Mead's drawings were dispatched to the EEG model shop for construction. \"The models for 2010 were probably the most complex ever built for a space film,\" commented Edlund, \"because the Leonov models had the rotating section. This made them more difficult to build, but at the same time made them more interesting to photograph. This was a stroke of genius on Peter's part, I think. Spaceships can get very boring to look at when you have nothing but a machine against black space with stars and a few planets. You very quickly run out of interesting visual dynamics. By having the living quarters of the Leonou rotate, the visual impact of the shots greatly improved.\" Working under the supervision of Mark Stetson, the EEG model shop first set to work on the large-scale Leonou. \"The designing and building occurred over a period of about two or three months,\" Stetson recalled. \"At first, we had a problem in finalizing the model due to the resolution of Syd's drawings -making certain that Peter liked the detail. That required pretty close supervision on Peter's part to make sure that he would be happy with the finished look of the models. There were some real frustrating moments for us. We would put a lot of work into something, assuming that we were moving in the right direction from what we could see in the sketches and drawings, and then learn that Peter's visions were entirely different from ours. As a result, a couple of portions of the model had to be reworked in a really major way - but fortunately, all in the pattern stages. Peter perceived the problem, and saw that he wasn't happy with it before we went all the way into the parts production - particularly on the crew quarters elements. Peter didn't like what we were doing on that and had us do it again. It was really painful for the people involved at that time, but it obviously paid off - the model turned out looking really nice.\" Stetson and his crew began on the Leonov by first constructing a three-foot-long mockup of the ship out of foam core. Once that was approved, they built a full-size mockup on which to base the pattern designs that would cover the outer shell of the model. From there, two different versions of the ship were constructed. \"For the most part, we used geluton for our pattern work,\" Stetson explained. \"Geluton is a real fine-grain wood that's just a little bit harder than balsa and it carves really easily. It's commonly used for vacuforming patterns and things like that. For some of the areas of the ship, we did a basic wood shape with geluton and then covered the entire thing in a skin of styrene on which we could plant detail. We went to the trouble of making a solid wood core so that when we made our molds and cast our parts, we wouldn't have any distortion of the flat planes. Generally, most of the ship was made off our molds, so we'd take an RTV mold off one section and then lay over it epoxy fiberglass or polyester fiberglass, depending on which part, and assemble it from those parts - the hard way.\" The model itself was constructed in essentially five sections, beginning with the bridge. \"The bridge was one of the parts Peter didn't think was quite right when he saw it coming to a stage where he could really evaluate its shape relative to the rest of the ship. As a result, he asked Syd to redraw it with a lot more detail; and so the ship became a lot busier at the very front. The next major part was between the bridge and the docking mast - with the antennas on top and the docking arms at the bottom. That forward section was one epoxy lay-up casting. Then the docking mast itself was treated as a separate section. After that came the core of the crew quarters - all the rotating members which actually begin slightly behind the docking mast. That was a fabricated part rather than a molded and cast part, and was constructed all from flat stock. Then came the rear section with the cooling fins and the engines. Those were the major pieces. As for the minor pieces and detail - of those there were countless thousands.\" Besides the outer detail of the ship, which was largely refined by Mead in his many drawings and sketches, the overall length of the Leonov model also had to be determined. Based on references in Clarke's novel, the Leonou was determined to be slightly less than half as long as the Discovery, an idea which Hyams wanted to maintain. As a result, when the model of the Discovery was built, it was redesigned slightly to accommodate this relationship. 'We essentially ignored any major changes in"],["13","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 13 •Dob EXIT"],["14","2010","14 • CINEFEX 20"],["15","2010","the proportions of the model,\" Stetson continued, \"but with regard to the T-bar bridge, however, we had an element connecting the two ships physically that required some modification of the Discovery.\" Before such alterations could be made, however, the original scale of the Discovery had to be determined, as well as its detail, a research assignment that went on as both models were being constructed. While Thomas Pahk commenced work on the mockup of the Leonov, Leslie Ekker began playing the role of detective, hoping to rediscover the Discovery - a task more difficult than anyone anticipated. One reason was because there were discrepancies between the length of the ship as reported in Clarke's novel (seven hundred feet) and the detail work on the original 2001 model which altered these proportions somewhat for effect. Also, the original drawings for the Discovery were nowhere to be found. \"We contacted Tony Masters while he was working on Dune,\" Stetson elaborated. \"He was the original production designer on 2001, and he said that to his knowledge, all of the drawings were destroyed. We already knew that the original models had been destroyed, and during our research, we heard a lot of interesting stories about why. Some said that Stanley Kubrick had a pique over being called back from principal photography in London to finish the editing in New York before he was really finished shooting. And so the story went that he had the models ruined so that M-G-M would not be able to use them in any other films. What Kubrick told Richard, however - which, to me, sounds much more likely - is that the models were left in England in storage and that finally M-G-M didn't want to absorb the storage charges for them anymore and decided to destroy them. At any rate, the only source left to us was the film itself. First we viewed it on videotape to find out what scenes we wanted to use for reference, and then Virgil Mirano went through a nice, clean, unprojected 70mm print of it reel by reel. We set up a little rig on his enlarger that allowed him to actually enlarge single frames into big prints. We then placed tracing paper over these prints and did proportional checking by applying rules of perspective to determine the proportion and how things lined up throughout other portions of the ship. In the end, we basically had to redesign it. Les did all that work, and did a real nice job. We got the basic proportions first, then the first layer of detail and the basic components of the construction; and then, as we were doing the pattern work on the various parts, we would use different frame clip blowups to copy the detail from the original model. As a result, our replica was fairly authentic. There were parts of the ship that were even a little more detailed than on the original, and there were other parts of the ship that you never saw in the first film, so we had to assume a symmetry or do whatever we wanted, depending upon the physical requirements of the model.\" Ultimately, the final ship dimensions were dictated by Richard Edlund. Basing his decision on the size of the shooting stages and the size of the available matting backgrounds, Edlund proposed that both models be twelve-and-a-half feet long. A smaller, six-foot Leonov - in scale with the larger Discovery - and a Leonov-scale half-model of the Discovery would also be constructed for use in the docking sequence late in the film. Large by contemporary standards, the twelve-and-a-half-foot Discovery was still miniscule when compared to the fifty-fourfoot monster fashioned for the original film - but then, that one didn't have to be tumbled end over end. \"Models don't need to be that large anymore,\" Stetson asserted. \"Lens technology is a lot more advanced today than it was fifteen years ago. They can do as much with modern lenses and optical work as they did the first time with a much larger model, and I think we did a good job of reproducing the detail on that fine of a scale. In terms of the physical shape and the perspective on its size, it's real close to the original - although it is actually less than a quarter of the length of the original model. The difference in the surface finish, of course, didn't allow for as much panel breakup detail, so we had to add a bit more surface detail. We also gave the model a real odd-quality look by covering it with sulphuric deposits and voicanic debris that would theoretically be ejected from lo.\" The Discovery model had a total of ten mounting points - five in the spherical command center at the front and five in the aft engine section - so it could be mounted from different positions depending on the camera angle. Because of its size, it also had to be mounted from both front and rear to be properly suspended. Otherwise, if mounted only at the center, the model would sag at the ends. \"Originally, the plan was to cover the unused mounting points with sections of the hull which were attached to the basic structure via magnets,\" said Stetson, \"but this did not always work satisfactorily, so we found it better to leave the armature doors sealed during photography. When one mount point was used, the model was cut open to reveal that mount, then later resealed when we wanted to use a different point. Whenever we could, we tried to lump together all the shots requiring one given mounting point, but that was not always possible.\" Both the Discovery and the Leonov also included several points of articulation. On the small Leonov, only the rotating crew quarters was articulated. On the large one, however, considerably more was possible. \"The main antenna panned and tilted; the grabber arm telescoped, rotated and grabbed; the back and side covers of the engine sections could swing out: . and the outer banks of the engines inside each also rotated and swung forward. In all, there were ten articulations on the model, each activated by a little stepping motor built into the model itself - most of the time, anyway. In one shot, we had to come up with an extra articulation on the small Leonov because we had to show both ships physically linked together and the small one was built to scale with the large Discovery. Since the shot required that the grabber arm rotate into its normal position after it had separated from the Discovery, Bob Spurlock came up with a nice little solution by simply cutting into the side of the model and running a small belt drive to the grabber arm Before principal photography could commence, video imagery of the two ships and their planetary environs had to be generated for display on the closed-circuit monitors that permeated the Leonov's live-action sets. Executing the model photography aspects of the assignment were effects director of photography Dave Stewart and video engineer Clark Higgins, who recorded the effects footage directly on tape with a prototypical Sony stop-motion system. / Stewart (left) observes as his crew previews a camera pass for a close flyby on the Leonov. Three motion control stages were used continuously throughout the production. / Manning one of the facility's 65mm stage cameras, motion control technician David Hardberger prepares for a dramatic down-angle on the Leonov. CINEFEX 20 • 15"],["16","2010","dispatched from the Leonov readings indicating possible from the Voyager missions, effects art director George Michelle Moen to perfect the globular representations of lo and Europa used during the effects photography. / In the Leonov data bay, Russian probe's passage over Europa. EEG's model photography on the right monitor is echoed Video Image - in the person of Greg McMurry and Rhonda responsible for engineering and operating the elaborate An unmanned probe is to investigate sensor lifeforms on Europa. / Referring to NASA photographs transmitted Jenson works with artist cosmonauts direct the by computer readouts - devised and executed by John Wash and Richard Hollander of Video Image Associates - on the left. Gunner - was also on-set playback system, often involving separate video feeds to twenty or more monitors at once. 16 • CINEFEX 20 shaft and hanging a stepper motor out the side of the model on the off-camera side. This saved us from having to do the shot stop-motion and, after the shot, we simply repaired the detail on the model. It worked out real well. Bobby did a lot of great rigging stuff on stage for this film, including articulating the Discovery antenna.\" In addition to the models of the two main spaceships, Stetson and company also had to build the Leonou probe. This tiny craft - which had to be stored compactly, but then open up into an array of panels and sensors when launched - was eventually stop-motion animated in three shots by Randall William Cook. \"The probe was a very intricate model,\" Stetson noted. \"It had very delicate wings on it, and the hinges had to be located where you couldn't get access for any kind of mechanical animation. So Randy came in and looked at it and said, 'No, that's impossible: but I'll try anyway. And so, naturally, we fought back and forth about how to handle each particular joint on it - we argued because of time and he because of convenience, which meant time for him of course - and ultimately, I think he might have gotten the short end of the deal. We ended up constructing a bicycle seat stool so he could sit with the model and get slid in and out of the bluescreen setup to make his adjustments. It was a real pain for him, but it ultimately was the only way to get the shot. There were about forty separate movement points on the model to make the wings unfold and to make the antennas telescope and unfold. The animation looked pretty nice and, in the end, was real smooth and steady.\" The probe was required for an early sequence in the film, when the crew of the Leonou detects signs of life on the frozen moon of Europa and sends the remote-controlled craft to investigate. The surface of the moon itself was actually a large tabletop miniature approximately forty feet long and twenty-five feet deep, photographed by effects director of photography Dave Stewart (ASC). Initially constructed from plywood, the surface of the miniature was covered with plaster, styrofoam ice forms, paraffin and mica flakes. For distant views, Europa was represented by a three-foot sphere authentically painted to match Voyager photos. In other scenes, her volcanically active sister satellite, lo, was either a three-foot sphere or a closeup eightfoot-wide dome section. Creating Jupiter was not nearly so straightforward. Since the turbulent, multi-colored planet was the thematic and dramatic focus for much of the film, both Hyams and Edlund realized from the start that the success of the special effects effort would depend largely upon how well the planetary giant could be realized on screen. Compounding the usual problems of creating a believable planet was the fact that in recent years the Voyager space probes had sent back extraordinary pictures of what Jupiter really looks like - visual images that would somehow have to be adhered to in order to make the Jupiter in 2010 seem like the authentic article. To solve these problems, Hyams and Edlund sought out a completely new approach from any that had ever been used before to create a planetary image - an approach that incorporated the latest in not only special effects technology, but space exploration as well. \"I think more time and effort was spent on physically getting Jupiter to look like Jupiter on this film than has ever been spent on any single image in the history of motion pictures,\" Hyams commented. \"Initially, I began to investigate all the possible ways we could do it. We obviously started thinking about possibly doing it in bands and manufacturing each band separately by means that included everything from cloud tanks to animation. But soon it became obvious that the only way to get the kind of resolution and specificity we needed was to synthesize it digitally.\" To accomplish this, Edlund enlisted the services of Digital Productions, whose principals - John Whitney Jr. and Gary Demos - he had first encountered during The Empire Strikes Back when, under their former Information International banner, the pair had successfully produced an X-wing fighter as a test of their digital scene simulation process. Later, when Triple-l began waivering on its commitment to film work, Whitney and Demos formed their own company, procured a Cray X-MP - the world's most powerful computer - and soon landed a plum assignment producing all the spacecraft effects for The Last Starfighter. It was during that production that Richard Edlund first contacted them with regard to possibly participating in 2010. \"During our first meeting,\" Demos recalled, \"Richard Edlund, John Whitney and I went over and talked to Richard Terrile and a couple of other JPL people, and they showed us the different images they had of Jupiter from the Voyager spacecraft. The most striking was some 16mm footage showing the clouds on the planet moving over time and making fabulous swirling motions, but the films were all choppy and broken up. There was bad data in them and dropouts, the images got brighter and dimmer, and just basically all the kinds of problems that happen with satellite transmissions. Anyway, the first idea we were asked to consider was whether we could take these images, patch them up and make them into something. Considering the fact that for a feature film the images really had to be relatively spotless and flawless, it looked at first like an impossible task.\" Also on hand during the initial meeting were Digital Productions software developer Larry Yaeger and fractal consultant Walter Gish. \"It was Richard Terrile,\" said Yaeger, \"who came up with an early idea to take the JPL data and do a frame-by-frame interpolation - adding more frames in between the existing ones by linearly interpolating between points, basically. But in fact, as it turned out, even the mere resolution, coupled with gaps and artifacts in the JPL data, made it impossible for us to use it even as a starting or initial condition, much less make it work for every frame over a long period. Walter was thinking about some kind of fractal approach to statistically describe color distributions and the like and come up with something that looked like Jupiter. But that also didn't really meet the"],["17","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 17"],["18","2010","18 • CINEFEX 20"],["19","2010","criteria, because we didn't just want it to look like Jupiter. We wanted recognizable, known features - we wanted to see the great red spot and we wanted to see the giant bands in motion. Then the other aspect was that this was really a motion problem, not just a 'look' problem. And since my background is in numerical fluid dynamics, I suggested using the basic picture to create particles and then employ fluid dynamics to advect the particles every frame and then rerender them back into this picture texture which gets mapped onto a sphere, and you have Jupiter - flowing.\" Yaeger's suggested approach was a significant departure from Digital's customary technique of encoding three-dimensional shapes and then moving them around in computer space, but it was one which fit nicely into some long-term goals Demos and Whitney had already established. \"From the very beginning of Digital Productions,\" Demos explained, \"one of the things that John and I wanted to do with supercomputers was to put a large amount of our computing power not just into the rendering of an image, but into the underlying structure of how it behaves and how it moves. The modeling of physical processes and character behavior is an area that requires a tremendous amount of computing - almost as much computing, or sometimes more, than the rendering of a realisticlooking frame. But we felt, from the onset, that this was one of the next major steps in the industry; and for us, the Jupiter project seemed an ideal opportunity to get our feet wet in that area - to simulate actual physics and fluid dynamics. So we undertook to do a test for them.\" The objective of the test was to determine whether the fluid dynamics of Jupiter's turbulent surface could adequately be simulated by the Cray and, at least initially, did not concern itself with the equally thorny problem of resolution compatibility between the computer generated imagery and EEG's 65mm effects photography. Though produced in 35mm, the footage was not altogether without purpose. If successful, it would be transferred to tape and incorporated into the material being generated for the live-action video monitors. As is often the case, the Digital Productions team found that they had to solve nearly all of the major problems associated with the project just to produce a satisfactory test. Confident, however, that they could do the job, they launched into the test with the full resources of the company behind them. One of the first moves was to hire Craig Upson, an expert in mathematics and fluid dynamics, whose work in modeling air pollution patterns at the Lawrence Livermore Labortory paralleled the synthesizing of Jupiter's constantly shifting cloud surface. \"We started off with the JPL data,\" Upson explained. \"JPL had a whole series of mosaic images that they got back from Voyager I and Il that they had spliced together into what they call snakeskins - these long, cylindrical maps of the planet on a flat image. They had one of these skins for each rotation Voyager made around Jupiter and then kind of animated them so you could see the gross movement on the planet surface. We decided to take one of those images, which was a color raster image, and use that for our initial guess at the state of the clouds on the planet at that moment. From there, we could take the image and map it back onto a sphere so it would look like a globe again. But that was only part of the problem. The next was figuring out how to get the motion right. We did that by solving a quite complex series of equations that gave us the motion from frame to frame - basically the same equations that you use to predict weather patterns, which is really what you're doing on the planet Jupiter. The most difficult thing was determining how the winds are on the planet. Jupiter has a complex motion. There are bands of color that have a tremendous shearing effect as they go across the planet; and within those shearing bands, things are swirling around and grabbing onto each other, just like clouds do. So that was another problem that we had to do kind of phenomenologically, to some extent. For every new frame, we would calculate what the motion was on the previous frame and then move every little bit of cloud, so that frame by frame, the clouds would be set into motion. After that's all going, you kind of let it go and the computer marches off in its own direction - what it thinks the complex motion should be, based on all this data you've given it. You can speed it up or slow it down, but it's pretty much on its own as far as direction goes. You can't say, 'I want to start at frame 50,' because the computer has to kind of march up to it, Thus, using a single cylindrical projection mosaic from Voyager Il's 347th rotation of Jupiter during the spring of 1979, Digital Productions generated a low-resolution twenty-fivesecond simulation of the planetary giant, complete with red spot and shifting bands of color. Hyams was delighted, and ordered commencement of the next phase of the operation - producing comparable material with sufficient resolution to hold its own in the company of super-sharp 65mm effects elements. Meanwhile, the 35mm test went to EEG for incorporation into the video material. 'We got a lot of mileage out of that one shot,\" Richard Edlund commented. \"First we transferred it to video and then we blew it up, reduced it down, put false terminators on it - in fact, all of the plates of Jupiter in the video were done with that one shot.\" While the first test revealed an approach that would work well for the video monitors, a significantly more complicated process was needed to make the JPL information usable for the 65mm shots. \"It turned out that the JPL data was just much too coarse,\" Upson conceded. \"It was fine for scientific purposes, but it certainly wouldn't hold up on a large screen. Used just the way it was, it would have looked terrible in shots with the models and the live-action. The motion was great, but the image itself was simply too fuzzy.\" Edlund agreed. \"The footage was acceptable for video, but the resolution was about on the level of a 16mm image - maybe a little less. Obviously, it wouldn't have been enough to go along with our incredibly crisp 65mm composites. So, since the Mounted on a motion control boom arm, a Sony video camera sweeps over a tabletop representation of Europa to record a probe POV shot for later playback on the Leonov's video screens. A snorkel lens allowed the camera to handily skim the surface. / Clark Higgins monitors the Sony BVH-2500 stop-motion recorder. / Video segments featured on board the Leonov during the probe sequence were intercut with 'God's eye' views of the same action. For these, another larger-scale representation of the Europan surface was constructed and then photographed in 65mm for subsequent optical compositing with probe, sun and star elements. CINEFEX 20 19"],["20","2010","Modelmaker Jarek Alfer shouldered the primary responsibility for producing Europa's icy moonscape - an amalgam of plaster and sculpted styrofoam covered with paraffin and mica flakes. / Dave Stewart takes a light reading on one of the video setups. The shot, designed for use during the docking sequence, features a side-mounted Discovery command module and the Leonov's articulated grabber arm. / David Hardberger (left) uses a joystick mechanism to program camera moves on the Discovery while Dave Stewart and Clark Higgins preview the move on the video system's monitor. In addition to the Sony recorder and camera, the unit also employed an Ultimatte video compositing system to instantly combine spacecraft and planetary elements. 20 • CINEFEX 20 resolution on the data was not great enough, I insisted that Digital make us three side-by-side VistaVision-format frames from the JPL data, and then we joined them together by snipping the film and putting them in one negative carrier. From that, we made about an eight-foot-long cibachrome print and then we had Ron Gress go in with his magic airbrush and essentially repaint the entire snakeskin - which was quite a project, because there was a lot of detail on that surface.\" \"Ron is a genius,\" Upson affirmed. \"He spent a month repainting the entire thing. For reference, he got the videotape that we had from JPL and played it over and over again. We also gave him a bunch of books on the basics of what fluids look like and the type of swirling you would get in an atmosphere like Jupiter's, and he very carefully tried to integrate all of that into his work. Understanding the complexities of what makes vortices swirl is real tough, but Ron managed it and he did a wonderful job of repainting the whole snakeskin, using the detail that was there as a starting point, but adding much more.\" The JPL information provided Gress many details from which to draw his own work. In particular, he used some closeup shots taken of parts of Jupiter which were not incorporated into the snakeskins because those were made from more distant images of the entire planet. One aspect of the detail that was left completely to choice, however, was the color of the planet itself. \"No one knows what Jupiter really looks like, color-wise, because it has only been seen in infrared. So all the colors that you're used to seeing are really kind of pseudo-colors. Nonetheless, Peter Hyams had distinct ideas of what the colors should be, and so Ron used those.\" Once the painting was completed, it served as the initial piece of data from which the digital imagery would be generated, replacing the actual JPL material used in the first test. \"Our next problem,\" Demos explained, \"was deciding how we were going to get that high-resolution image into the machine. The ideal way would have been to scan 4x5 or 8x10 sheet film, but we don't have an 8x10 or 4x5 scanner. What we have is a digital film printer scanner which can scan VistaVision 35mm, but that's the largest format it can see. We could have built a bigger scanner, of course, but that would have taken a year or two to do, so obviously there wasn't enough time.\" Richard Edlund came up with a workable solution by having EEG staff photographer Virgil Mirano photograph the repainted snakeskin in a series of VistaVision frames. Because of the large size of the painting, no one frame could take the entire image in and maintain the desired level of detail. Thus, the snakeskin had to be photographed in a series approach, with all the VistaVision frames shot vertically on end and slightly overlapping each other. The frames were then scanned individually into the Cray - a process that normally requires several seconds per frame, but was lengthened in this case by the fact that successive but essentially identical frames were scanned and then averaged together electronically to eliminate film grain. Overlapping areas were also averaged together in a concatenation process that effectively served to splice the frames into a continuous uninterrupted image. \"We didn't know if the seams would show in between the different VistaVision frames when we did that,\" Demos noted, \"but Larry Yaeger wrote a program to splice them together that solved the problem. We then brought Richard in and showed him the spliced pictures and asked if he could find the seam. When he pointed to somewhere it wasn't, we knew we were in good shape. You couldn't see the seams - they were totally When the frames were successfully spliced together and resolution determined to be of the highest quality, Upson, Yaeger and consultant Robert Myers worked to refine the software needed to bring the still image to life. Working from a photographic print, Craig Upson identified each vortex in the cloud-like surface and then designated in vector form an appropriate direction and strength to the vortex. That was then encoded into the computer in such a way that each vortex would respond appropriately to the universal laws of fluid dynamics which were also being programmed into the system. \"There are three steps to the whole process,\" Yaeger explained. \"One is solving the equations of motion for every frame. The next one is moving all these little particles around every frame. And the third one is making an image out of it. All three are very time-consuming. Each frame is composed of something on the order of eight million particles. Each particle carries with it its location in the picture - designated in 'x'and 'y' terms - and its color in red, green and blue components. All those things are packed up in one 64-bit Cray word, and so for an eight-million particle image we have an eight-million word disk file that goes out on our Cray disk. Once those particles are established, you then use your fluid dynamics code to push them around on a frame to frame basis. The system reads through all those particles - all eight million of them - and uses the little velocity vectors everywhere to push the particles just a little bit and write them back out into a duplicate particle file, only moved forward one step in time. Then the system reads the particles and writes a new picture file which is then mapped onto a sphere for the next frame. And that's your planet.\" Complicating the difficulties in determining proper motion for the Jovian cloud surfaces was the fact that much of the physics behind those particular dynamics remains shrouded in mystery. \"We worked with Richard Terrile and the JPL scientists on exactly how the vortices should move and interact with the constant streams or banded layers passing nearby,\" said Demos. 'We also asked exactly what the latest theory was on what makes Jupiter look and behave as it does; and they gave us three or four, all of which were very different and sounded very off-the-wall. Scientific theories are sometimes very strangesounding, and these implied physical structures under the atmosphere that were very odd. It's generally agreed that Jupiter is a very deep, dense planet which is either in a gaseous or fluid state well into its diameter. Some scientists feel that it"],["21","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 21"],["22","2010","22 * CINEFEX 20 157 130 0 8204"],["23","2010","doesn't even get solid until the very middle, which may be as small as the earth. In any event, what's going on underneath is really what's causing the motion on the surface. We know that there are various gases that swirl around at different heights and have different colors and temperatures, but beyond that, scientists don't have the faintest idea of what's really going on underneath Jupiter's atmosphere. They do have a lot of ideas, but many of the theories radically disagree with each other. \"Using relatively state-of-the-art fluid dynamics code, there are major things that still cannot be accomplished based on the physics of today. People are designing spacecraft, airplanes and everything else with programs which have these problems in them. Most of the problems come from the fact that they can't afford to compute the real solutions, so they make compromises, and these compromises lead to certain distortions in what actually happens. We ran into a number of these things. For example, based on our first model of the planet, the red spot started tipping over with time, because it was a vortex and we had a swirl there. So we had to figure out how to get it to stay put while it was rotating around. To do this, Craig spent some time reading technical papers from JPL and PhD theses from Cal Tech, and eventually pieced together a working model. However, for other cases, in order to make the planet look reasonable, we had to essentially abandon some of the correct numbers for physics and put in numbers which were quite erroneous for things like viscosity, rate of motion and such, so that when the image moved, it looked good. If we had used the real physical numbers, the thing wouldn't have functioned like Jupiter at all, since what's really causing the movement on the surface of Jupiter is something under the atmosphere that we don't even know about. So we had to solve our probiems with a process that worked on a surface level only.\" Though strenuous attention was paid to simulating the natural appearance and movement of Jupiter's surface, aesthetic judgments dictated that certain liberties be taken with the digital imagery. For one, the motion of Jupiter was accelerated. \"Our mages were moving a couple of orders of magnitude faster than the actual wind fields on the planet,\" Upson admitted, \"but if they hadn't been, there would have been no point in doing it. In an eight-second shot, there would be no noticeable movement on the planet at all. So we had to speed it up just to get the effect. The problem was that we couldn't really tell how much to increase it until we put it on film and tried different speeds. The motion is very complex and we couldn't really predict what we were going to get without doing the computations first. So we put out some film and Richard would let us know whether he wanted it faster or slower. Determining the right speed was very tricky because, on the one hand, if you go too fast the equations become unstable and you get unpredictable results; and on the other hand, if you don't go fast enough, you don't get any motion. So it was a little hard to get it to look the way we wanted.\" Even with the basic problems solved, however, there were To simulate the turbulent, multicolored surface of Jupiter, Hyams and Edlund turned to Digital Productions, one of the front-runners in computer generated imagery. Working from Voyager data provided by the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Larry Yaeger and Craig Upson used fluid dynamics code to bring to life each cloud formation on the giant planet's evershifting surface. / Airbrush artist Ron Gress painstakingly retouches a giant photo blowup of the low-resolution JPL imagery, which was then rephotographed and scanned into a Cray X-MP supercomputer. / Digital Productions cofounder Gary Demos examines the digital film printer used to scan the material. / The shifting surface of Jupiter was set in motion, frame by frame, by the Cray and then mapped onto a sphere. Upson previews a portion of the Jupiter implosion on a Ramtek monitor. / To get maximum usage out of the computer simulation, most of the imagery was generated flat-lit so that each shot could be repositioned optically and dressed with different terminators. CINEFEX 20 • 23"],["24","2010","Spurlock - responsible for much of the model rigging stages - prepares for a shot aerobraking sequence. As the cluster of inflatable ballutes just forward of the engine compartment to slow the craft down and deflect the problems with scale, the accomplished in a series of quick inserts, with a more ballutes were fully deployed. aerobraking maneuver, the Leonov becomes a virtual ball of fire. To achieve such shots, a rigid foam mockup of the ballutes - with an covered with pyrotechnic material, mounted on wires or placed in front of giant fans, and then ignited and Key effects man Bob on EEG's motion control early in the Leonov ship enters Jupiter's atmosphere, it deploys a heat. / Anticipating actual inflation was rigid structure being substituted once the / At the height of the aluminum replica of the Leonov attached - was filmed at high-speed. 24 CINEFEX 20 production difficulties. For one, the process was taking too long. \"It started out as something like eight to ten minutes per frame,\" said Upson, \"which doesn't seem like much, but most of our shots ranged from eight to twenty-five seconds, and when you're talking about twenty-five seconds of simulation, it takes forever. Some of our early scenes required two or three days just to get the shot out, and when you're using everything the Cray's got, it slows the rest of the company down to nothing. As configured, the Cray is actually two equally large computers, and each of them has about a million words of memory. Off to the side, we have another eight million words of memory that it has access to - and then there're millions and millions of words on disk, slow access. When this program was running, it went into both of the computers and started grabbing information from everywhere - on the order of forty millions words for every frame - which is why no one else can really do this kind of work. You have to have an awful lot of memory, massive amounts of disk storage, very high-speed information transfers and the fastest number-cruncher around.\" Still, the Cray's ten-minute per frame cycling time was proving problematic. Ultimately, the solution came in having Mitch Wade and Larry Yaeger translate portions of the complex program from user-friendly Fortran to machine-friendly Cray assembly language, which speeded the process up to the point where the limiting factor was no longer the computer, but the 65mm camera that had been rented for the project and installed to record each completed frame. \"We do our filming off a CRT face,\" Upson continued, \"about five inches across. Since the image is in black-and-white, each frame requires three passes, one for each of the primary colors. The amount of light that comes off the CRT face determines how long you have to expose it, and with the lens and filter system we had on this show, it took about two-and-a-half minutes per frame. So when we got our computer time down to that, we were definitely within the Resolution was another recurring problem. \"Even after all the steps we'd taken,\" said Larry Yaeger, \"when we put some of our early stuff on 65mm film and projected it onto a big screen, we could still see some graininess. So I came up with a software technique we call 'nonlinear picture texture' which allows for an arbitrary mapping of the resolution of the image onto the planet. In essence, what that means is you allow yourself to have very high resolution in the portions of the planet that are directly in front of you and very obviously visible, and you let the resolution relax as you go around the limb where you have foreshortening. And you take advantage of that. So as the picture wraps around the back side of planet - well, we don't compute all the back side anyway - but whatever little bit of overlap we do to make sure we don't have trouble with the edges, we let go to very coarse resolution. And that was the final kicker we needed to get the look right.\" Excluding the test footage, Digital Productions generated only eleven images of Jupiter, even though the planet would appear in dozens of different shots throughout the film. The key to making this work was Richard Edlund's initial determination that by generating the footage flat-lit, the imagery could be altered in the EEG optical department and used over and over again without necessarily betraying that fact. As an example, terminators on the planet surface - where light slipped into darkness - were created optically, and by changing the position of the terminator, the entire image could be made to look different. Also, the position of Jupiter in the frame could be altered and the whole image could be cropped differently. The most challenging synthesized sequence came in the climax to the film. \"At the end of 2010,\" said Upson, \"when the monoliths suck in all the material of the planet towards it center, we needed an effect to make it look as though the black spot was growing. What we did was add a sink term to our fluid dynamics calculation that said particles are attracted by some kind of magnet near this center and they just kind of flow into this big black hole that's growing as the planet begins to shrink. At the same time, the shape that we mapped our surface detail onto - which for normal shots of the planet had been a sphere - now had this big indentation in it that was also growing at the same time. By doing this, we created a black hole effect with everything swirling around and creeping inside the planet - very much like water flowing down into a drain. It required a very complicated motion - everything just going away into infinity.\" Unlike the basic flat-lit Jupiter footage, the black hole scenes were lit for effect on the computer, with significant input from Richard Edlund who worked side-by-side with the Digital staff during this critical phase of the production. In the end, Digital Productions' ground-breaking work on 2010 was a significant advance for the computer animation field - and one that more than satisfied the exacting demands of both Peter Hyams and Richard Edlund. \"That image represented something that the human eye had never seen before,\" Hyams commented. \"At JPL, the person I worked with the closest was Dr. Terrile - who, coincidentally, recently discovered a new solar system. But for Voyager, his job was to do part of the mapping of Jupiter and lo, so this was his turf. And I can only say that when he saw the image, he was moved by it. It literally is every single cloud formation mapped properly and moving independently of each other. It is beyond a simulation - it is Jupiter, and it's an image to be proud of.\" Meanwhile, back at EEG, the modelshop was still under a full head of steam, producing new miniatures to meet the effects shooting schedule and modifying others that had already been completed. One particularly challenging assignment involved the Leonou volutes - the large balloon-like bags that slow the ship down as it swings around Jupiter to establish its orbit. The volutes - which required a slight reconfiguration of the Leonov after the basic model was already completed - were, as with other aspects of the ship, designed by Syd Mead. Initially, however, the braking system was to take a much different form."],["25","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 25"],["26","2010","26 • CINEFEX 20"],["27","2010","\"We knew that we had to go into Jupiter orbit,\" Mead explained, \"and Peter really wanted to have the ship go in bridge first. He wanted the dramatic opportunity to have this 'thing' permanently stowed between the two halves of the bridge, have it come out forward of the ship and then open up like a big iron umbrella. It would then set itself with braces, and during reentry there'd be all these sparks and wonderful ablative glows coming off the front of the ship. So I designed this big, incredibly over-engineered-looking knuckle that would control all these plates, because if they fold all together like an umbrella, they've got to unfold and you've got to fill all these triangular sections in between the oblong plates. So I did it, and we were satisfied that we had a really neat setup for this.\" However, despite Mead's and Hyams' enthusiasm for the design, it was essentially shot down by Richard Terrile, 2010's technical advisor. \"He told us: 'First of all, just from the sheer standpoint of efficiency, you would use your own engines for braking.' He did say, however, that there was a way to use an inflatable volute, which is a carbon-fiber material, and you just inflate this big bag near the rear of the spacecraft and then use your rocket thrust and the bag itself to brake your ship. So with that idea in mind, I designed an add-on to the Leonov which had to be in place then for the whole journey.\" Once the design was finalized, it next had to be built and incorporated into the already existing Leonov model. For Stetson and his crew, this proved easier said than done. \"The ballutes were very difficult,\" Stetson noted, utilizing the terminology adopted by the effects crew for what looked like a cross between a balloon and a parachute. \"They were essentially a pneumatic gag, and that's like working with water in miniature - things often just don't look right. The way surfaces respond to air is something that the eye is instinctively attuned to, much the way it is with water and, as a result, it can be a real scale-blower. So we tried to think in terms of doing the effect without really dealing with the air, like semi-flexible things controlled by wire pulls. During the actual deployment, though, we did inflate them using air and shot them as very fast and tight inserts. We also decided to wait to show them completely until the ship had already started its entry into the edge of Jupiter's atmosphere. That way, by the time you see the ballutes fully deployed, they would be mostly obscured by the violent interaction between the ship and the atmosphere. Hiding the scale was a difficult problem for us, and there were some times when we just had to go ahead and do the shots - all of us hoping that they would turn out all right.\" The ballute deployment marks the beginning of the Leonov's first swing past Jupiter to establish an orbit near the lifeless Discovery, During this sequence, the Leonov appears to be a flaming fireball blazing through the giant planet's upper atmosphere. To create this vision of the future, EEG mechanical effects supervisor and master pyrotechnician Thaine Morris employed a technique he had devised for a film about the very beginning of the space program - The Right Stuff. \"For the With Jupiter looming above it, the Leonov slips into orbit around Io. At Peter Hyams' insistence, all space shots were photographed with a single key light and no fill - a factor which heightened the sense of realism, but at the same time dictated a new approach to Edlund's traditionally-employed bluescreen techniques. / Held in stasis between Jupiter and lo, the Discovery tumbles end over end as the Leonov and her international crew approach with the intent of stabilizing and boarding her. / Cameraman John Fante lines up a shot on a small miniature of lo's volcanic surface. / One of Bob Spurlock's most difficult rigging assignments involved mounting the twelve-and-a-half-foot Discovery on a rotating device for its tumbling CINEFEX 20 • 27"],["28","2010","The Discovery tumbles out of control between Jupiter and frontlight/backlight traveling matte technique was chosen over bluescreen for most of the miniature photography models and the problem of blue spill with the single key light approach - some of the Discovery shots had to be reaccomplished with setups because of difficulties perfect-fitting matte. / In the technique, the model is first lit and photographed against black. Then, in a separate silhouetted against a white - due to the size of the jerry-rigged bluescreen involved in tumbling the model with sufficient precision to achieve a frontlight/backlight matte pass, the model is lo. / Although a screen or card. 28 1 • CINEFFY 20 entry of the Leonou into the atmosphere,\" said Morris, \"we used reduplicating fluid - a mixture of nitrocellulose, nitroglycerine and several coloring agents that we got from MP Associates. It's very flammable and you put it on as a liquid and it dries out like paint - real thick stuff. We called it 'pyro-goop' on The Right Stuff. Then you light it and it burns down real fast. You can get it in red, blue, green and white. We used the red and put it on the ballute, which was about two-and-a-half or three feet across. The coating was a little over a quarter of an inch thick, and that would give us about a five-second burn.\" Depending on the particular shot, the goop-coated ballute model was then filmed by cameraman Bill Neil in one of two ways. In the first, the ballute was ignited and then dropped from an eighty-foot crane, with the ground-based camera following it as it slid down a long wire strung from the top of the crane to a point on the ground 120 feet away. \"The buildings we have aren't really high enough or big enough to get crazy with that big of a fire inside,\" Morris commented, \"so we shot these scenes at night out in our parking lot where there would be no danger of setting anything on fire. We ended up with a pretty good size flame.\" The crane shots were filmed at speeds ranging from 48 to 72 frames per second - using a custom 65mm high-speed camera devised by EEG chief engineer Gene Whiteman - and because the goop created a flame unlike that produced by a normal carbon fire, the usual problems of scale were not readily apparent. The second approach involved a ballute-configured Leonou model mounted on a stationary pipe rig. Once ignited, the goop started to burn while large fans blew the flames back and away from the model. The camera would then dolly past the model to create the illusion of the ship soaring through the Jovian atmosphere at a high speed. \"The ballute itself was made out of foam, with high-temp epoxy on it, so it could take the heat,\" Morris continued. \"Those were usually good for four or five takes. On our first take, though, the dummy ship behind the ballute caught fire and burned. When it did, it changed the flame characteristics so that scale went all to hell. I was surprised that it got that hot back there behind the ballute. We thought the ballute would shield it, but apparently the fans weren't pushing enough air past it. I'd done a similar burn before using epoxy and had no problem, but the styrene that the ship was made out of just couldn't take it. So the modelshop had to make another one out of aluminum and that worked out fine.\" Although the modelshop was busy throughout the production, a lion's share of their work had to be finished within a few short months to meet a shooting deadline that was actually well ahead of the start of principal photography. On most films, the special effects shooting usually extends way into postproduction and rarely, if ever, begins until the script has been finalized and live-action photography commences. But on 2010, the requirements were quite different, primarily because Peter Hyams wanted all the video display screens on the spaceship to have real images in them. \"There is nothing worse to me than burned-in images,\" Hyams noted, \"and the kind of photography that I do doesn't lend itself to burned-in images.\" As a result, the preparation of video effects material for the Leonov monitors - some of it very specific - became EEG's first and highest photographic priority. Rather than film the material and then transfer it to video, it was determined that the most satisfactory results could be obtained by working directly in the video medium. To supervise the technical aspects of this effort, Edlund and Dave Stewart - who did the videography - called upon Clark Higgins, an expert video technician whose eclectic background included light shows and multimedia presentations in Seattle, soundtrack recording on Apocalypse Now, plus the design of Francis Coppola's electronic editing system on One from the Heart and the ILM videomatics system used on Return of the Jedi. Included in the footage that was needed were point of view shots from the Leonov and her probe that showed sections of the Leonov itself, the derelict Discovery spacecraft, as well as Jupiter and her moons. Since standard video is recorded at an unvariable rate of 30 frames per second, customary motion control techniques - which are dependent upon movement over time within a given frame of film - could not be used to create, in video, the kinds of fluid model moves audiences have come to expect. Nor could the moves be captured in real time. \"Often,\" explained Higgins, \"Dave wanted the camera to move over the model very closely, and if the move had to be in real time, the vibration of the system would have been unacceptable. We would have had all sorts of bumps and bounces. Also, when they were shooting the Leonov, the crew quarters would rotate at a speed not fast enough for what they wanted in real time.\" The only suitable solution was to shoot in stop-motion. \"In video,\" Higgins continued, \"there are not a lot of systems for doing stop-motion animation. And those that are around are pretty time-consuming. Normally you have to pre-roll, roll down, do an edit of a frame, back up, do another edit of a frame, back up, and so on and so forth - a six-and-a-half or seven second process for each individual frame. Well, for the volume of material we needed, that would have taken forever. Fortunately, about the time we were gearing up for this project, Sony came out with their new BVH-2500 recorder - where all you have to do is press a button and it records immediately. And really, our ability to even do this project came out of the fact that Sony very graciously agreed to help us out. Not only are they one of the highest-quality video manufacturers in the world, but they also have a vision towards the future - as demonstrated in their high-definition video systems, among other things. Anyway, the Sony 2500 does not have to pre-roll or anything. Basically, it records a frame, steps forward immediately to the next, and all you have to do is press a button to record again - up to four frames per second. In essence, it reduces the amount of time necessary to record a sequence by as much as fifty times. To do a twenty-second shot with"],["29","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 29"],["30","2010","30 - CINEFEX 20"],["31","2010","individual edits could take as long as ten hours. With the 2500 machine, it required maybe twenty minutes at the most.\" Though standard motion control equipment such as tracks and stepper motors were employed to impart movement to both models and camera, each segmented exposure was recorded instantaneously on videotape without the characteristic motion blur deemed so important to film effects. \"Most stop-motion video is recorded in frames,\" Higgins explained, \"which, especially if things are moving, will have an annoying strobe effect. The Sony 2500, however, can record fields on a sequential basis, as opposed to frames, and the results are beautiful - like nothing you've ever seen in video stop-motion. The reason for that is that each video frame is composed of two fields. One field is all the odd lines and the next field is all the even lines. When interlaced, the fields produce a flicker rate of 60 cycles per second instead of 30 cycles per second. At 60 cycles per second, there is no perceptible flicker within the video image. If you just did 30 frames per second that were continuous frames, you would see a definite flicker, just as you would if you projected film at 24 frames per second. That's why, when film is projected, each frame is shown twice - so you're actually running at 48 frames per second. Anyway, by breaking the video image up into sixty individual segments - 60 fields per second - as opposed to 24 or 30, you get much smoother motion and it's the same as having a blur there.\" In tandem with the Sony 2500 stop-motion recorder was a Sony BVP-330 - a standard broadcast-type video camera light enough to be held on the shoulder. In this case, however, it was mounted on a motion control pan and tilt head and used much the same way a film camera would be, complete with a snorkel lens that allowed very close access to the models. For Dave Stewart - long accustomed to working in 65mm - the video project afforded a pleasant opportunity to see exactly what he and his crew were shooting, as it was happening, and to know immediately whether or not it was satisfactory. \"The camera was much smaller than a 65mm camera,\" Stewart commented, \"and very much lighter. In fact, I think the camera weighed something like fifteen pounds - a little less than one of our 65mm magazines. Plus you don't have to load it with film that adds even more weight. The Sony system was terrific. The only problem was we couldn't get it right away, so we started out with a Lyon-Lamb for a week or so while we were waiting. It was Sony's first 2500 in North America - a prototype - and they didn't want us to have any downtime on stage. As a result, they wouldn't bring it in until they felt it was perfect.\" As with most visual effects assignments, the video footage for 2010 required the use of an effective matting system; and for this, Higgins selected the Ultimatte. \"The Ultimatte is probably the best video matting device in existence right now. You can do a lot with it that in film you would have a hard time doing - things where you would end up with garbage mattes and lots of other problems. But with this electronic process, you have the ability to see what you're doing in real time, to see any flaws you may have in your blue and to use electronic means to clean up some of those flaws.\" The Ultimatte was used primarily for shots involving more than one ship in the frame or when multiple elements were required. \"We created the background plates first and then put the foregrounds in front of them. For instance, there was one shot where the Discovery had to rotate with Jupiter in the distance and lo in the foreground. So we first created the background of Jupiter and lo, then added the third element of the Discovery rotating in front of it. So that was a threeelement shot - most of them were no more than three or four elements deep.\" Conveniently enough, background starfields were deemed unnecessary. \"It was the director's choice not to put starfields in the footage. Having looked at the material coming back from NASA, he saw that, in reality, stars don't show up anyway. Also, it would have made the shots much more complex and would have required that every single space shot be an Ultimatte shot. And because of the size of the models, we would have had to use enormous starfields which would have also had to move. So it was decided not to use them.\" In seven weeks of shooting, some forty shots - approximately thirty minutes' worth of footage - were produced on EEG's makeshift video stage. \"Some were simpie and some were complex,\" Higgins concluded, \"but all were done much faster than we expected. With the Sony 2500, we were able to do two or three versions of some of the shots and give the director his pick.\" Much of the footage produced would become incidental background material for use on the live-action sets. Other more specific shots, however, were components of elaborate and carefully planned narrative sequences. \"Our main storyboarded sequence was the one involving Europa and the probe sent down to see if there is life on the moon. Most of it was shot from the point of view of the probe, using a large model of Europa with the motion control rig skimming over the surface. The sequence begins as the probe leaves the Leonou, pulls back, turns and zooms down to get closer to the surface of the moon. Then, as it skims over the surface, it sees a crater, turns around to investigate, goes over the top of the crater, sees something there and, as it does, is destroyed. That final part of the sequence involved about eight or nine shots done with multiple small models.\" All of these shots, used exclusively on the Leonou monitors, were intercut with 65mm footage photographed and composited much later. The creature itself, a nebulous energy form devised by Gary Platek, was a separate project created in the facility's cloud tank using a mixture of various dyes. Meanwhile, an equally ambitious adjunct to the video production was taking place several blocks away at Video Image Associates, a small independent company comprised of video engineers Greg McMurry and Rhonda Gunner and computer graphics experts John Wash and Richard Hollander. Former associates on a number of Douglas Trumbull productions - most significantly Brainstorm where, individually, they had created the film's computerized video readouts and devised a As Heywood Floyd (Roy Scheider) helps engineer Walter Curnow (John Lithgow) into his pressure suit, one of the Russian cosmonauts - suspended from an overhead wire rig - walks down the rear wall of the Leonov's zero-gravity pod bay. / On stage at M-G-M, actor Elya Baskin floats in front of a blue flux screen. Devised by Richard Edlund, the blue flux system employed a front projector and a high-gain Scotchlite screen to create the necessary blue field behind the spacewalking performers, without the attendant blue spill a more conventional backing would have produced with the single key light approach. / Using hand thrusters - with a cel-animated exhaust effect - Curnow and Maxim Brailousky propel themselves across the void separating the Leonov and the tumbling Discovery. CINEFEX 20 • 31"],["32","2010","Three operators - including performer, via wires attached spacesuit. Since the wires gravity-bound spacewalker could pivot into a variety of Brailousky connect with the two astronauts make their module and scale its outer continues to rotate. Since the set piece on which they stationary, the sensation of movement was suggested by crane-mounting the lights and swinging them through postproduction, a spinning starfield and planet would be actors. / Stagehands prepare Lithgow and Baskin dangle one overhead - were required to 'fly' each to either side of a leather harness worn under the were connected at the actor's precise center of gravity - adjusted to include the suit - by moving his limbs, the seemingly weightless positions. / Curnow and tumbling Discovery. / The way to the command surface as the ship were climbing was the shot. Later, in composited behind the for another shot while in front of the blue flux screen. 32 • CINEFEX 20 mobile 24-frame video playback system - they had, with the subsequent de facto breakup of the Trumbull organization, formed Video Image Associates with the intent of doing similar work on other films. Shortly after M-G-M announced its planned production of 2010, McMurry wangled a meeting with Peter Hyams and, on the strength of a heavy-duty reference from Douglas Trumbull, walked away with a contract for all the video graphics and video playback work in the film. With only sixteen weeks before commencement of shooting, Wash and Hollander launched immediately into the production of readout material while McMurry and Gunner concentrated on developing a large-scale mobile playback system that would allow for maximum flexibility and responsiveness on the set. \"Richard and I were generally responsible for the overall technical scheme of the movie,\" said McMurry. \"We knew we had to produce a lot of good-looking graphics in a short period of time, so we opted to take the first part of our schedule to write software for creating graphics, rather than just try and start producing graphics right away. That way, we kind of built up a head of steam. Peter wanted a low-resolution look to the graphics, which was fortunate because we wouldn't have been able to get anything else in volume in the amount of time we had. A lot of the work we did looked like vector graphics, in that it was largely line drawings, but everything was actually done on a raster graphics system - actually an IBM PC with a Cubicomp frame buffer. We always tried to make the best-looking image we could, and if John - who was our main 'artistic' eye - felt that the design of a particular image would be better with a solid on it, or something like that, then we had the capability of doing shading or building solids. Sometimes, if we wanted to highlight a particular section of the Leonou, we might fill solids in on that one section and then the rest of the ship would be in wire-frame form. So we used it as an artistic tool, rather than for any sense of realism. \"We digitized everything we could get our hands on - the Leonou, the Discovery. We even digitized a head for a medical scene. We created about six tapes for use in the medical bay, and one of the things we wanted to have in there was a body or a head or something. We settled on a head, but we utilized a rather unorthodox method of doing it. We had a faceted styrofoam head - which was actually left over from Brainstorm - and we marked every point on the head and numbered every polygon. Then we went in on a lathe that was calibrated so that we could move a pointer over the head and measure the x-, y- and z-axes for each point. We did that for one whole half of the head. Next, we hand-keyed all those points into the computer, and Richard then realized the other side of the head from the first side, so there was now a full data-base for a human head. When that was generated as a filled solid, it looked identical to the original head, because both were polygonal. From there, it could be rotated or moved around.\" Wash and Hollander also had to create computer text in three"],["33","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 33"],["34","2010","34 - CINEFEX 20"],["35","2010","different formats. First, there was the English-language material to be used on earth during the opening scenes of the film. Second, there was the material to be used on the Discovery, which was designed to mimic the graphics look of 2001. And last, there was the Russian-language material that would be employed exclusively on the Leonov. The latter, not surprisingly, proved the most difficult. \"For the Russian graphics,\" said McMurry, \"John and Richard had to create Russian fonts and then relabel the keys on their computers with Russian characters so they could write Russian. We weren't given any specific idea of what kinds of Russian text were wanted, except for a few things that Peter had specifically in mind. So John and Richard went through the script and tried to extract as much terminology as we thought we might need, and we gave that to the production office and they had their translator work it out for us. At the same time, we went to the library and found a pretty large volume of appropriate technical terms that were translated in Russian. So we made every effort to make sure that the Russian would be right. Even on the set, I was often asking the Russian actors if what we had was correct - and I was very pleased, in most cases, that we hadn't made any real blunders.\" Although much of the Video Image material would be nondescript background filler, certain scenes - specifically during the Europa probe sequence - would be prominently reatured and had to correlate closely with the motion control videography then underway at EEG. \"The way Peter envisioned the Europa probe sequence,\" McMurry explained, \"was that everyone would be crowded around two monitors on a very cramped bridge - one monitor having the live-action view being sent back from the probe and the other one having the computer simulation, timed exactly to it. One way we could have done it would have been to feed EEG's live-action video image into the computer and then colorize it or something. But we thought the real thing would be better, so we decided to do a computer model of the whole planet surface and do a simulation. Ideally, we should have had the EEG footage before we began, but because of the time crunch and the delay involved in getting the Sony stop-motion system up and running, we had to start generating our mathematical model of Europa before Clark and Dave ever started shooting the motion control video work. Otherwise, we probably wouldn't have finished in time, especially since our footage took just as long to generate as theirs did - maybe a little longer since we didn't just have a model to rake our camera over. Every frame had to be generated out of computer memory. We began by having Virgil Mirano take a lot of pictures of their model surface. Then John and Richard broke the surface up into quadrants and digitized all the little sections so that they had in their computer a model of the planet surface they were going to shoot. From that point on, all they had to do was plot their computer moves to move right along with what was being shot at EEG. \"We showed our first piece of graphic animation to Peter after about four weeks. The second one came out maybe a week later. Fighting centrifugal force, Cumow repels down the side of the spinning Discovery. / Modelmaker Tom Silveroli adds surface detail to a clay sculpture of cosmonaut Max Brailousky. An early plan to use articulated motion control miniatures for some of the spacewalk shots was ultimately discarded in favor of using only live-action blue flux material or - for extreme long shots - tiny figures, ranging from three inches down to threesixteenths of an inch, photographed on the Oxberry animation stand. / After gelling the Oxberry lights to the required shade of blue, animation supervisor Garry Waller mounted the minute puppets on a motioncontrolled needle which allowed for movement in several different axes. / Although most of the figures required no articulation, some of the larger ones were equipped with wire armatures, enabling Waller to stop-motion animate the arms and legs, if desired. / Waller used a similar approach to animate the floating pens used by Heywood Floyd to illustrate his plan to escape Jupiter's gravitational pull - ahead of the launch window - by linking the two ships together. CINEFEX 20 • 35"],["36","2010","Discovery as seen through bridge and background ship in this shot were rendered in Chandra (Bob Balaban) steps out of the Leonov hatchway and onto the shuttle bridge that will transport him to the now-stabilized Discovery. Leonov exterior, a full-size set was constructed on stage mounted on a Louma crane maneuverability, follows the action. Working in very tight quarters throughout much employed the Louma almost The T-bar bridge and the open airlock leading from the Leonov pod bay. Because of the difficult perspective and depth of field requirements, the matte painting form by Matthew Yuricich. / Dr. For this section of the at M-G-M. / The camera, of the film, Peter Hyams for maximum exclusively. 36 • CINEFEX 20 The third one came out a couple days after that. Well before we even started shooting, we were turning out three to seven a week, and we continued all through the production. I think it would be very conservative to say that we generated over an hour of specific animated graphic effects for 2010 - probably a lot more. And John and Richard did it all - just the two of them. We did hire some assistants to shoot at night what was produced during the day, but basically all the shots were created and designed by Richard and John.\" While John Wash and Richard Hollander labored over the computer imagery, Greg McMurry and Rhonda Gunner channeled their energies into developing an appropriate on-set playback system. With some two hundred video monitors scattered around the various sets, the task was a major one indeed. \"Wherever we were on the ship,\" said McMurry, \"we were almost always feeding at least twenty monitors at a time. We had a wardrobe trailer that was set up on the stage, and inside we had fifteen videotape machines. The system was set up in such a way that Rhonda was able to operate all the equipment in the trailer while I was out on the stage working with the director. We set up controllers so that she could cue up all the machines simultaneously and replay them from the same point, time and time again, as they started each shot. It's very expensive to have everybody standing around on a set waiting for someone to cue up tapes - far more expensive than it would be, even, to put a separate operator on each machine - but the system we put together was pretty much all automated. Rhonda could do it all by herself. We had scenes where she'd be changing cassettes as fast as she could get one out of the machine and pop another one in, but generally it wasn't quite that hectic.\" Only 24-frame video was employed on the set, thereby sidestepping the rolling raster bar problem created by shooting video's standard 30 frames per second at film's 24 frames per second rate. While the Video Image graphic material had been generated straight-out in the film-compatible format, the model photography produced on EEG's video stage was recorded at the more conventional video frame rate and thus needed to be converted by Image Transform before it could be used on the set. Once in proper format, the 24-frame video could then be played out on specially modified tape machines which interlocked with the Panavision film cameras - much as a process projector would. \"Wherever we were on the ship,\" McMurry continued, \"we were almost always feeding at least twenty monitors at a time. Of course, we couldn't have more tapes than we had machines, but in some places it was appropriate to have the same signal. For example, on the Leonou bridge, the two pilot positions were pretty much mirror images of each other - with a little radar scanner that we had to feed and then four or five other tiny monitors. In that case, it was very appropriate to have them matched up, but we still had ten or twelve monitors to deal with. A lot of the camera work was done with a Louma crane, where the camera is floating around and the operator is panning remotely, and in those cases we didn't necessarily know which monitors were going to be in the shot. So we literally had to light up the whole end of the ship whenever we did any filming. Obviously, we couldn't be directly wired to all of the monitors all of the time, so we set up a series of trunks and a cable harness system that would allow us to repatch things very quickly. Thus, we could feed one line from each machine out to the set; and then, out at the set, our portable distribution package would allow us to split up the feeds to whatever number of monitors we wanted. Since most of the monitors were heavily built into the sets, most of them were prewired to come to this common point. That way, we were able to change things around from shot to shot in less time than it would normally take them just to change the camera setup. \"Our whole objective in developing this system was to minimize the number of times I would have to say to the director, 'We can't do that.' Peter, of course, had a lot of stuff in mind way ahead of time. But often he'd come on the set with a new idea that could have easily thrown us if we hadn't built a maximum amount of flexibility into the system. To allow for those sorts of things, we had set up a complete portable minipostproduction editing facility in our trailer so that Rhonda could take all the elements we had generated at the shop and recompile them - change the order around, stretch out a sequence, shorten it, whatever - right on the spot. Using more conventional video techniques, we could even recomposite images if something needed to be changed. For example, one of the predominant images was a computer-generated picture of Jupiter - but it was only twenty seconds long. Well, nobody can shoot a scene in twenty seconds, so we had to extend that. And although many of the Jupiter shots already had terminators on them, we sometimes had to add our own by using a soft-edged circle wipe. By laying that over the image and changing the softness of the edge and the symmetry of the circle, we were able to depict the planet in different positions relative to the sun. Even the scenes where the black hole starts to form we did right there on the stage, moments before. So we always tried to be prepared for anything Peter might throw at us.\" With the production of the video footage well underway, Peter Hyams continued to polish his screenplay while production designer Albert Brenner supervised the completion of the numerous interior sets needed for the film. Brenner, whose credits include Hyams' Capricorn One, as well as such earthbound projects as The Turning Point and The Goodbye Girl, used his background in apartment designs to create the Leonov's bridge, navigation center, ward room, crew quarters, pod bay, hybernation chambers and medical bay, galley and numerous airlocks and corridors. In addition, he also had to reconstruct several of the original Discovery interior sets - in particular, the flight deck, the pod bay with its one remaining EVA pod, and the glowing red chamber containing the brain of HAL. Just as Mark Stetson had learned while trying to rebuild"],["37","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 37 ME"],["38","2010","38 - CINEFEX 20 Беля"],["39","2010","the spaceship model, none of the original sets or plans existed, so Brenner and his staff had to work from enlargements made from a 70mm print of the original film. Although the basic layouts for the Leonov interiors were devised by Brenner, much of the detail work was the result of Syd Mead's input. \"Once Al had established the floor plans in terms of size and to accommodate the camera angles Peter was planning,\" Mead explained, \"I was given some of the set drawings and asked to overlay their basic shapes with specific detail - like gauges, television cameras, readout screens and switch rows.\" While much of the detail Mead created in drawings of the interior sets was conceived to look very futuristic and space-oriented, one particular detail was not. \"Peter asked me to include a periscope in the design. The whole mentality of the film was of being in this container, this very close set of quarters, and Peter toned it deliberately to create a compressed, claustrophobic look. To further enhance this feeling, he said: We need a periscope. Think up a periscope that will look logical in outer space.' So I put together a classic periscope assembly of something coming out of an overhead surface, established a console work station with an eyepiece off to the side and a calculator keyboard with manipulator buttons. And it came off as a periscope - but an astral periscope where they could look and find stars and do their astral navigation with a hands-on fixture.\" Mead's periscope, no doubt, was one of the reasons Brenner came to refer to the Leonov interior settings as \"Das Spaceship.\" Once the interior set designs were approved, the task of their actual construction fell to special effects supervisor Henry Millar, who also added a lot of additional surface detail as his crew completed the settings on two of M-G-M's largest soundstages. \"We worked from the artist renderings,\" Millar recalled, \"but since they were somewhat sketchy, we had to do a lot of building just out of our heads. The drawings gave us dimensions and the basic design of the space: but beyond that, we just had to try different things until we could fill up a whole board and make it look like something. It was really kind of crazy for about the first two months, because we had to try a lot of different things until the sets had the look that Peter Hyams wanted. Then it involved a great deal of designing for the wiring and electronics end of it. At one point, we had fifty-nine men working on the sets, just in my department alone. \"It took us four months to build everything. We started out working ten-hour days, but that soon went up to twelve. We had a regular assembly line going, with one group doing the electronics and another doing the fitting and building. Every one of the buttons and knobs on the set had to be individually cut out of plexiglass, because they had all different shapes, and then all the plates that went around the switches also had to be cut out of aluminum.\" Consoles featuring these switches were lit from behind by fluorescent lights. In addition, 12- and 24-volt practical lights were built into the sets so that little or no additional lighting would be required during Hyams' Unlike most of the shots in the film, where the models were photographed as separate stage elements and composited optically, the ships' connection by means of the T-bar bridge required that both be photographed at the same time. To accomplish this, the twelveand-a-half-foot Discovery model was mounted abreast of the smaller, six-foot Leonov replica, and connected via a miniature bridge. / An early attempt to film the T-bar bridge in a water tank proved generally unsuccessful. / As hostilities escalate between the United States and the Soviet Union, the American astronauts are ordered off the Leonov. Miniature figures were used for scenes on the T-bar bridge. / Mechanical effects supervisor Thaine Morris at work on the bridge rigging."],["40","2010","Chandra enters the HAL 9000 brain vault in an attempt to astronaut Dave Bowman. / The camera, mounted on its Louma crane, records actor Bob Balaban entering the chamber. To impart a sense of weightlessness, Balaban was placed in a rigid harness and attached to the end of a long rotating pole which extended into the vault. / When escalating hostilities on earth force the Americans and Russians to repair to their own respective ships, the T-bar bridge is jettisoned monolith, hovering over the two earth craft, is a matte painting. In all, about thirty rendered solely in shades of black - were produced by Matthew Yuricich and his assistants, Michelle Moen restore the computer's higher logic functions, disconnected nine years earlier by Discovery into space. The giant monolith paintings - and Tanya Lowe. 40 - CINEFEX 20 characteristic low-light photography, which added greatly to the impact of tight and somewhat dark quarters. The interior lighting was also boosted by the large number of video monitors that adorned the sets. The bridge set, originally designated for only a few, eventually ended up with nearly thirty such monitors. One of the most difficult sets Millar had to construct was the Leonov pod bay. Unlike the Discovery, the Leonov pods were stacked vertically, and during a key sequence, one of the pods had to be lowered down to the floor and then moved out of the bay via a remote controlled tracking mechanism. To achieve the required lifting force, Millar employed a heavy-duty industrial forklift that was dressed and incorporated right into the set. \"The track was built similar to a roller coaster track, with two rollers on top and four underneath, so there would be no way it could possibly jump off. We had a shot that I thought was pretty good where we brought the pod down off the forklift, set it on a turntable which rotated it 90 degrees, and then had it travel along the track straight out through the opening bay doors, turn 90 degrees again, and then travel all the way out of the set. Each of the different directional transitions required a separate set of tracks, so we actually had three. The pod itself would break contact with one and then make contact with the next. We had a bit of trouble with that, but we finally got it to work out.\" Another troublesome assignment took place in the Discovery pod bay when Walter Curnow (John Lithgow) and Maxim Brailovsky (Elya Baskin) board the disabled ship and the Russian removes his helmet to test the air. Since the onboard heating units have been deactivated, his breath is immediately visible in the frigid atmosphere. \"Trying to get the effect of coldness in his breath was quite difficult,\" Millar continued. \"It turned out that the temperature didn't have that much to do with when we could see his breath - it was the humidity. Eventually we got the temperature down to 34 degrees and then by getting the humidity up to about 98 percent, we were able to get plenty of breath. But it was kind of shaky there for a while, because we kept lowering the temperature with no effect and we weren't sure why.\" To attain the proper levels of temperature and humidity, Millar effectively built a cocoon around the set with viscaline plastic and then brought in large refrigeration units. \"We had about a six-inch air void between the outside of the bag and the back of the set, and we ducted the warm air out of the top, recirculated it through the cooling units and blew the cool air into the set through the bottom. There was a great deal of heat loss overall, but basically it worked well considering that it was just a down-and-dirty thing we came up with to avoid freezing the whole sound stage.\" Millar also had to build the Leonov bridge set so the front end could be elevated during the ship's escape from Jupiter's gravity. To do so, the set was constructed on a platform attached to a gimbal mechanism that could pivot the set 90 degrees. The gimbal, constructed out of four-inch square metal tubing, weighed close to three tons when completed. Designed to go in only one direction, the entire rig had to be counterweighted because the front of the set - loaded with numerous video screens and electronic gear - proved to be much heavier than the back. A braking system, built around the axle of a semi tractor and some air brakes, brought the set to a safe stop at the Before even the first frame of film could be shot, a uniform photographic approach had to be devised for both the liveaction and the visual effects. Again, Peter Hyams had a very definite idea of what he wanted - based, as before, on his concept of cinematic reality in deep space. The repercussions, from Richard Edlund's point of view, were significant. \"The Star Wars films were fantasies,\" Edlund commented. \"All the spaceships were brightly lit and nobody cared where the key light was coming from. The action moved so that the ships were just lit from whichever side made the models look the best. There was almost no backlight in Star Wars - we used all fill light on the models. In 2010, on the other hand, the whole picture is backlit. That's Peter's style of photography and that was the style that we wanted to carry through on the miniatures. But the fact that there is no fill light in space presented us with a major problem. When you photograph miniatures with our traditional bluescreen process, you have to have a fill light in order to wash out the blue spill on the models. But real space is mostly black and lightless, and we had to take this into account. Since the main source of the light on the ships in space would be from the distant sun behind them, we lit our models from behind, leaving the fronts of them mostly in darkness.\" \"I don't believe in fill light out there,\" Hyams elaborated. \"Until someone shows me differently - or at least until the end of this movie -there's only one sun in the sky. That, to me, means one light source - which put tremendous restrictions on Richard Edlund and his people, and really forced them to alter the whole process they usually go through to produce special effects. The most elaborate sequence in the film, in terms of length and what actually happens during it, is the spacewalk. And in order to do the flying work, a great deal of bluescreen was necessary - but bluescreen work simply would not apply. When I told Richard we'd be using a single key light approach, he said, 'Well, you have to put a little fill light in there to overcome the blue spill.' And I said: 'No. I don't accommodate how I'm going to light something for an effect. You're going to have to accommodate the effect to how this crazy person is going to light it.' And Richard, being Richard, came up with the solution.\" The solution, as it turned out, required a major reevaluation of Edlund's normal modus operandi. \"The rear-lit bluescreen technique has certain problems with it, photographically - that is, it requires you to use white fill light on your models to eliminate extraneous blue spill that is often reflected off certain parts of the models. As a result, it tends to dictate a key and fill light situation in space, which is not realistic. We've all seen the"],["41","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 41"],["42","2010","42 • CINEFEX 20"],["43","2010","NASA footage and we know that out there there's just one key light and no fill, unless it happens to be light reflected off some surface. So I started thinking about utilizing a front-projected blue system for the models and the actors, as opposed to using a blue screen. That way, we wouldn't have to get into using fill light. So we started experimenting with front-projected blue and then I found out that Apogee had, for another purpose, built their own front-projected blue system. Since their equipment was already available, we were able to rent it from them and then spend a couple of months tweaking it out and modifying it to our needs. We called it a 'blue flux' system - I think they called it 'blue max' - and we used it to shoot almost all of our spaceman wire work over at M-G-M. So, that system was really the basis for our look on 2010, which involved a lot of negative space. There is a lot of blackness in the shots, with finely chiseled backlit miniatures carving their way through it. It gave us the opportunity to create a different look; and it gave Mark Vargo the opportunity to produce some of the finest composites I've seen on the screen. It's a more realistic look, and since 2010 was not a fantasy movie, we couldn't take poetic license as often or as profusely as we did with Star Wars.\" Production for 2010 officially began on February 6, 1984, a full twenty years after Arthur C. Clarke and Stanley Kubrick had first discussed their ideas for 2001: A Space Odyssey. Among the earliest sequences to go before the cameras was the spacewalk, which not only inaugurated the blue flux system, but also involved extensive wire work. While many actors openly detest such semi-stuntwork, John Lithgow found the experience generally exhilarating. \"The big central sequence where I and my Russian counterpart do an untethered spacewalk from the Leonou to the Discovery is what really attracted me about the whole project. I thought it would be wonderful, because people are fairly familiar with weightless spacewalks from news footage and everything, but those walks are always performed by super-trained and efficient and problem-free astronauts - nothing ever really goes wrong, In 2010, my character is an aerospace engineer, but the interesting thing about him is that although he's a rocket expert and a troubleshooter, he doesn't know anything at all about being an astronaut. As a result, during his lengthy spacewalk, he's panicked by the whole operation. So there I was again, afraid of flying - sort of a 'Nightmare at Several Million Miles' type thing. Once Curnow reaches the Discovery, he clings on for dear life and is covered by this yellow sulphuric powder, so he gets filthy dirty. So the whole sequence created this feeling of a tactile, realistic man in space - what it would really be like up there to be a mechanic, not an astronaut. A working stiff in space - that's what we did.\" Even before the three-and-a-half weeks devoted to shooting the spacewalk began, Lithgow and fellow actor Elya Baskin logged several hours on the 2010 wire rigs, getting acclimated to the strange sensation. \"I took to it immediately. Everyone was very proud of me. I just loved it. It was like the ultimate The Starchild's brief appearance proved to be the most labor-intensive three seconds in the film. After an unsuccessful first attempt to render the infant figure in conventional foam latex, Lance Anderson devised a mixture of gelatin, glycerine and water that closely duplicated the translucent quality of human skin. / Primary head and body moves on the first Starchild were accomplished hand puppet-style by Teresa Burkett. Mike Hosch, who sculpted the baby, drapes the base of the platform with black velvet prior to an early film test. / Stuart Ziff engineered the Starchild's sophisticated eyeblink mechanism. / Bob Johnston devised a radio-controlled articulator which enabled the eyes to track and focus. / Modelshop supervisor Mark Stetson examines the inner workings of the second Starchild. Unlike the first, the approved Starchild was operated solely by cable and rod mechanisms. CINEFEX 20 • 43"],["44","2010","To gain possible new insight into the enigmatic monolith, Brailousky exits the Leonov Though a full-size version of the probe was constructed for use in the Leonov pod bay, the flying scenes were accomplished with a twoand-a-half-foot articulated cameraman John Lambert viewfinder as motion control programs a move and effects adjustments to the model. / Richard Edlund, Mark Vargo and Gene Whiteman examine Optical supervisor Vargo - tasked with producing some 350 exacting composites for 2010 - worked closely with Whiteman, who engineered responsible for devising its high-speed 65mm camera. in a manned probe and approaches the sleeping giant for a closer look. miniature. Effects watches through the technician Mike Hoover technician Robin Kolb makes last-minute a 65mm film element. the facility's primary composite printer. EEG's chief engineer was also amusement park ride. I got up there and did somersaults and banking, and they would shoot me down from one end of the stage to the other. In fact, I was ready to show off like crazy once the camera was moving, but they had to tell me: 'No, John. Calm down. When you walk in space, all you do is hang there like a sausage.' So I had to considerably cramp my style.\" Flying supervisor Robert Harman, whose expertise and equipment have been utilized in the Superman series and countless other productions, oversaw the extensive wire work. George Jenson was also on hand during most of the shooting to insure that any logistics-based compromises that needed to be made would not degrade the compositional qualities of the final shot. In all, three operators - each controlling a different axis of movement - were required to 'fly' each actor. \"Bob Harman and his people really have it down to a science,\" Lithgow continued. \"Each of us had a custom-made leather harness that went from about mid-thigh to our sternum. The harnesses were made up of long thin plates with several holes punched in them, and the people who caused us to fly around would just find the right holes to attach the wires to - at our exact middle point. This had to be done once we had our outfits, because the harness and suit had to be taken into account as part of our weight. Basically, you're suspended from two pivot points, and when you're hung by the wires, all you have to do is lift your arms and you'll swing forward, tuck in your knees and you'll swing upside down. It's all in the way you hold your limbs.\" Lithgow's enthusiasm was dampened - but only slightly - when the first unit launched into the protracted spacewalk shooting schedule. \"The suits we were wearing were real space suits built by real space suit makers, so they were designed to be worn weightless in space - and they weighed about a hundred we of course he pounds. Of course, we weren't weightless on Stage 30, so it was very uncomfortable after a few hours of it - just unbelievable. Fortunately, they would often bring us down. Unfortunately, with those harnesses on, we couldn't sit, and standing was just too uncomfortable in those huge heavy outfits. It was like carrying two great big bags of groceries for three hours. So we worked out a way for them to rotate us and lie us down on our sides. The wires would let us down all the way and we would look like these knights in armor that had been knocked off our horses. We would just lie there until they needed us again, and then they would hoist us back up. That was the only way to rest, and we really needed to rest because it was so strenuous. The longest, I would say, that we had to stay up there without a break was about a half an hour. But we would continue doing this material for hours on end, and we would get to the point where we were crying out, 'Let us down!' It was pretty uncomfortable. On one of the shots of Elya and me tethered together and floating through space, Peter decided he wanted a second angle, but to do it conventionally would have meant moving the blue screen all the way around and moving the whole huge camera assembly all the way around. To avoid having to do that, they decided to turn the camera upside-down and turn us upsidedown, too. And believe it or not, it was actually a relief to play the whole scene floating along upside-down, because it took all the weight off our thighs for a while.\" Despite the fact that Harman had a team of experts assembled to work with the actors, the wire work was still somewhat experimental because most of the moves had to be worked out on the set. \"It was a hit-and-miss process,\" Lithgow continued, \"because every one of the ideas Peter had to invent. We also had the added complication of getting the lighting right. In the sequence, we land on the Discovery's tail and then climb our way up to the dome; and the closer we get to the dome, the more centrifugal forces are supposed to be pulling us away. The set itself was actually stationary, so to make it look like the Discovery was going around in a circle, there were all these arc lights - designed to simulate the sun - rotating around on giant cranes. So the light source was constantly shifting, and the scene would go from dark black to scalding white as the ship supposedly rotated. As a result, though, it was very hard to figure out where the lights should be at any one time, and it was difficult to match closeups and things. It was not just a matter of getting lines right, but of being in the right position for the lines to coordinate with the light. We'd do a lot of takes, and finally get everything just right, and a helmet light would blow halfway through the shot. So adjusting all those elements for each scene was just incredible. As a result, we would get like one or two shots a day - it was so complicated. And it involved more waiting around than I ever want to do again in my life.\" Although most of the shots required the spacewalkers to hang on wires for extended periods of time, other approaches were sometimes used to suggest the illusion of weightlessness. \"We did every conceivable variation. We did closeups of me standing on a lazy susan, for instance, pretending to float around while some grip rotated this thing under me - like he was serving hors d'oeuvres, or something. We did other stuff where we just pretended to be weightless, and in this huge outfit I would have to sort of float and let my leg wander off. We also did one shot where we're climbing into the Discovery hatch. Elya climbs up to the hatch and suddenly I just float down from up above and drift inside. I did that with a stuntman hanging onto my legs; but the first several takes, I was just simply too heavy and I went crack into the side. So I had to summon all my strength to sort of ease myself in like Marcel Marceau.\" Cameras were also churning over at the EEG/BFC complex. Under the supervision of Dave Stewart, four crews were photographing the 2010 miniatures, turning out about one shot per day per unit. For Thaine Morris and his seven-man stage crew, the pace was exhausting, \"Since this was primarily a modeloriented film, and there were only two models in the film, there were a lot of changeovers and turnarounds. Basically, each shot required a separate rig, most of which were done by Bob Spurlock, who has quite a bit of experience as a model handler. We did a lot of strange and oversized rigging. The models were huge - the biggest I'd ever seen - and they required a lot of"],["45","2010","changes because they had to tumble and twist to be shot motion control.\" With 205 miniature shots, requiring a total of 823 elements, the job of efficiently scheduling people, equipment and stage space was especially important. \"In a space movie, obviously, no one is actually out in space with a camera, so everything is shot separately for the most part,\" said production supervisor tion and effects Michael Kelly, whose background as a live-action and effects editor afforded him a keen sensitivity to the importance of minute details. \"For maybe fifteen minutes of total running time, we have to produce - if we count matte passes - close to 1200 separate pieces of film. By comparison, Honkytonk Man - another film I worked on - was about a 700-scene movie, and that was two hours and two minutes. So in terms of what the realities are, producing fifteen minutes of effects is as fate more complicated as doing a whole live-action movie - maybe more so, because it's not just a matter of going in and getting the set and everything ready and then filming. It's a matter of coordination and communciation over a period of months - amine the mothe shooting a Leonou today that will be combined three months from now with a Discovery that was shot six weeks ago. So orce the storyboards are developed, we start analyzing them very carefully, shot by shot, determining what size model is to be used, whether it is to be coming toward the camera or going away - things like that. Then we try to consolidate all the shots that would benefit from having a particular model mounted in a particular way and try to shoot them all at once on one stage, rather than jump around and waste a lot of time on totally different setups.\" Although the blue flux system proved altogether satisfactory for the live-action work at M-G-M, it was not an ideal system for the model photography. \"It's a big, ornery, hard-to-handle system,\" Morris commented. \"It uses a real high-output xenon lamp with color correction filters - and a beam splitter that hangs on the camera. It works well, but you'd have to have one hell of a motion control rig to handle that big a thing! It would have been real difficult on the model stage, especially when you consider some of our light passes run up to eight hours.\" As a result, most of the traveling mattes for the models were accomplished using the frontlight/backlight system, which involves a minimum of two passes on the model - one frontlit against black and a second one silhouetted against white. The technique had long been standard under Trumbull and Yuricich, but to accommodate 2010's jumbo models, key grip Pat Van Auken constructed two new screens - twelve feet high by thirty feet wide. \"All they were,\" Morris continued, \"was seamless muslin that was stretched out and sized for the background material. Then we put white latex on them. Most people think that motion picture screens are fairly high-tech, but most of them are not. They're just a piece of cloth with a bunch of holes poked in it, and covered with a good quality white paint.\" The various rigs needed for the film were constructed primarily out of black gas pipe and pieced together with CINEFFY 20 → 45"],["46","2010","46 - CINEFEX 20"],["47","2010","scaffolding clamps. Of all the ones built, perhaps the most crucial was the rig used for tumbling the Discovery, which had to enable the model to actually rotate in front of the camera. \"Bob Spurlock designed that rig also. It was just a big 'L' with a couple of braces on it. On top of that, we put one of the big, heavy camera rotators. This kind of rig would normally have been flat on a track to rotate the camera in a four-foot diameter circle, but we put it on its side so we could rotate the Discovery on its side. The Discovery had to be mounted from each end because it was designed for zero gravity, and if you mounted it from the center like you would a normal model, it would have drooped. So it had to be picked up at both ends, requiring a big heavy brace connected to either end - one into the ball and one into the engine section. The rig itself went through several configuration changes and, by using it, we could turn the model any way we wanted to once we got it up in the air.\" In addition to the Leonov and the Discovery, the spacewalk sequence featured some less obvious model work in the form of tiny human figures employed as stand-ins in some of the long shots. Most of them, in fact, were so small that it was not even practical to photograph them on the motion control stages. Instead, animation supervisor Garry Waller shot them on the Oxberry animation stand. \"Originally, we were going to do a lot of those shots using roto mattes, but our roto crew really became too bogged down working on all of the spaceship shots and whatnot. So Mark Vargo thought that we should try a bluescreen approach on the Oxberry. As a test, I put some bluescreen material over the lights, wedged it the first time, and it was amazing - the first attempt was real close. So we thought it would work and we went for it. We ended up using probably four sizes of little guys - from three inches down to three-sixteenths of an inch - all of which were made by the modelshop. They were amazingly small and, considering the one key light approach in space, they were fairly easy to light. Overall, it worked really well.\" The idea to stop-motion animate miniature astronauts was initially suggested as a possible approach for the spacewalk later in the film where HAL's human alter ego, Dr. Chandra (Bob Balaban) gets caught on a tether rope in between the docked Leonou and Discovery, \"We did two shots where Chandra is pulled along on the tether after HAL starts the Discovery's engines to leave Jupiter,\" Waller elaborated. \"To do this, we had the miniature Chandra on a gimbal device to rotate him, so he'd appear to be moving this way or tilting that way. The way the Oxberry is set up, I have twelve axes of movement - north, south, zoom, et cetera - and with the gimbal, I can add three more. With all of these axes, I can spin something or tilt it a little bit up to camera. The gimbal is clamped to the bed of the stand and I ran some pan-bar motors that I wasn't using into it to make it move. I even actually put some juice in the motors, and it's all motion control. For the Chandra sequence, the puppet also had a wire armature inside it so I could move its arms and make it appear to be flailing around a bit. At the same time, I had a motion control move pull away from him. This kind of work involved actual stop-motion type movements, frame by frame, and I had to judge how far they were moving. The bigger miniature man was used for this shot while the smaller ones were used for the real wide shots during the first spacewalk sequence - the long shots of the guys as they move in from the distance and approach the Discovery. \"Since I was filming them all bluescreen, I stuck an airbrush needle into the tiniest guys, hoping that the bluescreen would over-value the thinness of the airbrush needle and the tip that was sticking into their backs, and it did. So we didn't have to articulate a mounting pylon - just do a general garbage matte for the whole thing. Some of the other shots that I did were shot on glass and wax in profile. The wax provided support so that the miniature spacemen could stand up on the glass. Those were shot backlit in silhouette, and then we would rotate the whole glass and have the base of the stand move and the zoom move so they would appear to be moving away from us. They didn't have to really be articulated because they were so far off in the shot. As a result, they just barely moved.\" Once Waller completed his animation, it had to be incorporated into appropriate background plates and intercut with the footage of the actors. Both the live-action sequences and the animated puppets required further animation via rotoscoping, to clean up certain aspects of the shots and to make them more realistic. In one instance, Waller's co-supervisor, Terry Windell, assigned Richard Coleman to cel-animate some of the tethers seen during the spacewalk. \"For that,\" Windell noted, \"we did mostly just the long shots - where the men were either shot live-action and then reduced and/or where Garry did the miniature men. There are about five or six of those shots. They were quite simple in terms of printing and technique, but the animation involved was fairly difficult because one of the ropes had to be left uncoiled. In the scene, Max leaves the Leonou with a coil of rope on his arm, lets it go into outer space and then takes his hand thruster and starts to shoot it in one direction, causing the rope to uncoil. Showing it do that proved to be quite a task to do realistically because we were talking about a line about the thickness of your hair, and the amount of motion in certain sections of it had to be so minute that it was almost, but not quite, tracing over itself. That's one of the hardest things to do in animation, because when the motion is minimal, you often get a vibration or a wobble. But Rich did a good job with it.\" The animation department aiso encountered numerous shots that required rotoscoping - particularly those involving the spacewalking astronauts where, for one reason or another, the figures had been shot against black rather than blue. \"During the spacewalk sequence, we had to do a lot of articulating of the men in front of what was originally to be a starfield,\" said Windell. \"That wasn't so frightening because a starfield is black and we just had stars to deal with. But then, during the course of making the film, things changed and we often had to articulate a person's entire body, frame by frame, in front of a bright The Leonov fires her boosters to get into position for the docking maneuver. Engine effects were a combination of practical lights built into the engine pods and photographed on a separate pass, top-lit airbrush artwork supplied by the animation department and a ripple glass diffusion effect added in optical. / Garry Waller works out a motion control model move on the facility's newly computerized Oxberry animation stand. / Using an animation field chart, visual effects editor Arthur Repola plots element moves for the spacewalk sequence. 47 CINEFEX 20"],["48","2010","The docking of the Leonov and the Discovery provided stage and camera crews with demanding challenges. Since spacecraft miniatures were twelve-and-a-half-feet long - even though the Leonov than half the length of the Discovery - a larger-scale Discovery was constructed for scenes requiring both ships to be photographed (right) and his stage crew some of their most both of the primary was supposed to be less partial model of the together. Bob Spurlock prepare an unusually complex bit of rigging. 48 • CINEFEX 20 orange lo or Jupiter. That was really tough stuff. Annick Therrien headed the rotoscoping section, and it involved a phenomenal amount of work.\" Another reason the rotoscope work was particularly difficult was because the single key-light approach - on both the spacecraft and the astronauts - made it almost impossible to determine the shadow side of the matte. \"We were literally animating figures, legs, pods, packs, half a spaceship - it was really scary. There were a couple of shots that posed quite a problem; there were dozens of them that were difficult: and there were maybe seven that proved to be next to impossible. They were really labor-intensive, but Annick managed to expedite them.\" Another area of major concern was the background starfields. Once again, since realism was the prime directive, the starfields had to be as accurate a representation of the universe as possible. Unlike a fantasy film where the starfields have no basis in reality, the 2010 fields needed to look like something that might actually be seen from an orbit around Jupiter. As a result, Garry Waller found himself trying techniques never before used on such an assignment. \"Unlike the usual white stars on black background approach, these were black stars done on white paper. To generate the fields themselves, I actually used black spray paint and stood about four feet away from a sheet of paper. I used spray paint instead of an airbrush because I knew we could get fifty different sizes of stars that way. Airbrushing has a controlled grain, whereas a spray can can't be controlled at all. Twenty-two inches was our basic paper size, although we got into some that were eight feet long for shots that had to pan rapidly. Those were done the same way. Usually I put the paper on the floor and sprayed above it, developing the right movement to get a starfield that looked good. Even though it was just simple spray painting, I had to go through a lot of paper in order to get about ten starfields that would work. I had to do some touching up also, mostly to remove the large globs of paint that would occasionally occur. Then Peter went through and picked the ones he didn't like, and we went with the rest. Most of them looked real good - crisp and deep like we wanted. Then we shot them high-con - black on white - in order to provide Mark Vargo in optical with an original element that he could just put in and print as camera-ready artwork. It saved a generation ultimately, and we didn't have the grain or contrast which sometimes eliminates a lot of stars.\" To create movements on the starfields, Waller filmed them on the newly computerized Oxberry. Because the fields were often to be composited with images of Jupiter or her moons, he had to plot the movement very carefully in order to match moves made on the planet and moons when they were originally photographed by Neil Krepela on the facility's Compsy system. \"Neil would film the planet on the Compsy, the planet would have a move on it, and then I would have to shoot the starfield so they could matte the planet over it,\" Waller explained. \"These were often blocked right out, like for some of the shots of the tumbling Discovery; or in one, they tilt down off the window onto HAL and you see Jupiter and the stars rise out of the frame - shots like that. There was one shot where the camera is looking out the windows and then pans with the actors. Shots like that created a lot of movement on the plates and the stars had to kind of follow that relationship. Those shots took many hours. One of them, in which the Leonou rises up into the frame, moves east and then slowly settles down on the Discovery to dock, took about ten hours just to plot out the moves. That was one of the longer shots, but it looked real nice when it was finished.\" Overall, Waller had to supply moving starfields for forty-four shots - considerably more than had originally been anticipated. \"There were a lot of starfields in that film. But then, there's a lot of space out there.\" During the spacewalk, the astronauts use little hand thrusters that shoot out bursts of gas to directionalize their movement through space. As with many of the shots in the sequence, animation was needed to supplement the action filmed on the soundstage. To create the look of realistic gas, Windell and Waller decided upon a traditional technique not often used these days - top-lit airbrushing. \"We introduced top-lit airbrush for the hand thrusters and for the engines,\" Windell explained, \"which was just something that we had not normally used. Over the years, we've probably just forgotten about it because bottom light has become the mainstay. However, for 2010, we wanted to get away from that real day-glow neon look that you find in Star Wars. Realism was being pushed here, so Garry suggested that we try some top-lit stuff and it worked very nicely. We could get a lot more subtle colors, for instance, by airbrushing colored gouache - which is an artist's paint that mixes readily with water - onto black exeter paper. Airbrushing against black provided a real nice texture. On each successive frame or cel, the airbrushed area would naturally shift a little bit, because controlling an airbrush density is very difficult, but we incorporated that movement so it would kind of flutter. That proved to work really well with the engine shots that had a light pass. The light pass would come on and we would add some top-lit vapor moving within that. From there we moved to the hand thrusters. On the set, they had shot them against bluescreen with CO, coming out of them, but as soon as the CO, came out it was contaminated by the bluescreen, which effectively eliminated it. So we went back in with airbrush, top-light again, and made layered sections of a burst. The burst looked like a flame with a green color on the outside and white on the inside. We just animated it, and the top-light approach gave it a more pastel look rather than the bottom-light, which tends to look more neon-like. The results are very nice, but the process does create some problems in the actual animation. It's harder because you have to paint on black exeter paper and you can't use the light tables to see your sequential drawings - you can only flip the drawings. But, for the type of stuff we were animating for 2010, which was mostly vapor and engine exhausts, that didn't matter.\" Once the Discovery has been stabilized and reactivated, the"],["49","2010","scientists and astronauts turn their attention to the real focal point of their mission - the monolith. Floating just kilometers away from the two ships, the mysterious black slab at first remains a silent giant apparently unwilling to reveal its many secrets. While the monolith in 2010 looks identical to the one in 2001, it was created for the sequel in a way quite different from the original. \"We chose to do them all as matte paintings as opposed to dimensional models,\" Edlund noted. \"Generally, the most difficult kind of object to light is a spherically-shaped one 1 But the - because of the problem of it reflecting light. But the second most difficult shape to photograph would probably be something like the monolith because, first of all, it had to be perfect. It could not have any flaws on its surface. That's one of the points brought up by Clarke in his novel - that the monolith is theoretically perfect. The thing is also described in the book as being totally reflectionless - but in a practical sense, you can't really show anything that's squarish without seeing tones. So George Jenson and our matte painting master, Matthew Yuricich, worked out a scheme of creating the black monoliths with paintings, showing a little bit of light here and a little bit of light there, with these little bits of light being just a few tones off black. It was a difficult task.\" \"It was very hard work,\" Yuricich concurred. \"We had about thirty monoliths to paint, and it was tough to get the look of Let than don opene them to balance properly. I think Peter Hyams didn't like the idea of using minatures because they would have been too shiny and sometimes they would have showed some texture. He wanted the monoliths to look shiny, but not real shiny, and to look like they would absorb light - kind of blackish, although some of the ones in 2001 were actually a little bluish-green. So we started painting them until we finally hit on a formula that he liked. Michelle Moen did most of the monoliths and it was a real labor. Because they really are just made out of different tones of black, the completed paintings never looked like much when we were done, but the blends would show if they were not quite right. We had to give some character to them - we'd try to give a little light to one edge and just the opposite to another to give them some composition.\" To create the look that Hyams wanted, Yuricich and Moen found that not only did they have to paint the monolith in shades of black, but they also had to paint it on a black background. \"We tried it once on white,\" Yuricich continued, \"but the matte showed a little edge around it. So we decided to paint black on black, and I had all the background boards painted black. Then, I would leave alone the area wherever the blackest part was and just paint the lighter, greyer area. Some of the first blacks that we painted were too flat so we had to use some oil to bring some gloss back. Trying to get enough gloss in there without it becoming too shiny was a real problem. We used one paint that looked almost like a mirror, and Peter didn't want that. We also tried using an airbrush, but neither Michelle or I really handle those very well, and so it just wasn't working out. We had another girl here named Tanya Lowe, who was an CINEFEX 20 • 49"],["50","2010","50 - CINEFEX 20"],["51","2010","assistant, and she helped out too because it was just brutal labor - not only labor labor, but you had to have a feel for the blend Starting each painting with one of the black-painted boards, Yuricich and his assistants would use white chalk to trace out the space the monolith was to occupy as specified in the storyboard for that shot. A matte was then creating by etching out the same area on a white-painted piece of glass. Then Yuricich and his associates would proceed to paint the desired shades of black onto the board. When the painting was later photographed by matte department supervisor Neil Krepela on the Compsy camera system, the camera would shoot through the shape scratched out on the glass and photograph the shades of black that had been painted on the board. \"When we painted the monolith, we didn't have to paint an edge. We just went past it, because the camera would take out just what it could see through the clear glass matte. \"Every black you get looks black until you put it against another black. Then one of them will look almost grey compared to the others. Of course, it's what the camera sees that is the important thing. We found that if we used one kind of darker black called ivory black, we could solve the problem of the images being too glossy. Usually, the painting boards would be a little darker in the center because we would use a little ivory black there. This helped make the paintings look wonderful and Peter was very happy with them, because it gave them a kind of funny absorbent look, yet a little satiny and smooth. If the monoliths were real, I don't think you would actually get the light effects we had to create. We had a black sky with stars, and unless the monolith was overlapping with Jupiter which is white, you wouldn't see the monolith at all unless we somehow created those light effects to give it some shape.\" To the untrained eye, the difference in tones would be almost undetectable, but the camera would always pick them up. \"Oh boy, did it. If our blend wasn't any good, it would show. It was a labor, but it had to be, requiring someone with some expertise and zing, because we had to see that blend. And we got real good at that after a while. As a matter of fact, it became almost impossible for me to paint those things because I could see the slightest little defect in the blend and I would belabor the son of a gun for a long time instead of just going out and making a test to see whether or not the camera would actually pick it up. Once we got the formula set, it was just work - we just had to go ahead and do them. \"An interesting thing about the monolith is that when we first started, in the script, Peter called for a ratio of 1:4:9 - 1 thick. 4 wide and 9 long. We did that and it looked like a cement block. I talked to Neil about it, and said: 'There's nothing to it - no aesthetics. It looks like a damned brick.' He said that was what Peter was calling for, but to go ahead and slim it down a little anyway - so I did. And as soon as Peter saw it, he said, 'Go ahead and slim it down. Actually, our proportion ended up being something like 1:4:15 or something like that, to give a little grace to it. We dug up some photographs that they had of the 2001 monoliths and, my god, those were about 1 to 401 Very slim. About half the size of a domino proportion. So even though it was written to be 1:4:9, everyone seemed to realize that it wouldn't have the grace and symmetry at that exact ratio.\" Eager to explore the monolith, the commander of the Leonov sends Max Brailovsky out in a manned pod, hoping that a closer look will provide some answers to the apparently sleeping giant. Instead, the monolith seems to begin opening up, revealing millions of light rays which converge at its center. To produce this effect, animation was required to supplement the monolith matte painting. \"I called those effects starstreaks,\" said Terry Windell, \"because they just appear on the face of the monolith, and they look like little shooting stars - hot points of energy that appear at the edges, shoot inward and then, as they reach the center, disappear. Peggy Regan did the animation for the little streaks, using the same basic approach you would use for a shooting star. First, you create the path you wish it to travel on and decide for how long you want it to travel. You chart this out and then make one long piece of artwork in black to trace the path of the starstreak. Then you make a negative kodalith of that which gives you a clear line on black. We call that piece of negative a reveal,' and it will just opaque out whatever we don't need. From that point, you just airbrush each individual starstreak on a cel. In the sequence, there were three shots where you actually see starstreaks, and in each shot there were maybe 150 starstreaks or something like that. Since each starstreak required about fourteen or fifteen pieces of artwork, there must have been at least a couple thousand pieces of artwork in all - which is quite a stack of cels. In the center, the starstreaks start building up a glow of energy - which was an effect simulated by Gary Platek with his laser, and which we then augmented. It turned out to be pretty tedious, in that it took twenty-minute exposures to create the glare we needed.\" Once the energy inside the monolith collects, it erupts in a huge fireball that explodes out and shoots off past the Leonou towards the earth. The approach used for this energy bolt was the same one used earlier in the film, when the unmanned probe is destroyed by an erupting energy bolt on the surface of Europa. \"It was meant to look like a comet coming straight at you,\" Windell explained. \"I did it on the plate-finish exeter black paper using bits of airbrush and hand-brush work, and actually airbrushed the ball expanding from one size to a size maybe four inches or five inches in diameter. From there, Garry was able to pull back on the animation camera and start his own piece of artwork, which was a dot of light that he tracked into and through until my last piece of artwork filled the frame. So, by using the camera and streak photography, he took the relatively short distance I animated in and made it seem to be traveling thousands of miles. Then, when it goes away and it's in the distance, it looks more like a comet, which Garry did mostly with his camera and less with artwork - it was kind of a moire To facilitate camera placement, both miniatures were stood on end for a shot of the Leonov grabber arm closing in on the Discovery. / Cameraman John Fante takes a light reading off the inverted, full-length Discovery model while assistant Bess Wiley makes adjustments to the motion control camera. / To assist the animation department in plotting appropriate moves for their starfield backgrounds, David Hardberger prepares for an additional camera pass on a shot involving the enlarged Discovery nose section, marked with strips of tape for ready frame-to-frame reference. CINEFEX 20 • 51"],["52","2010","John Fante takes a spot light reading off a setup showing the Leonov and the Discovery angle closeup on the two ships - shot against black. shots would be generated on silhouette the model. / Peter approach is most evident in this backlit view of the two Starfields were fashioned in the animation department by spraying specks of black Then, by shooting on highstarfield footage could be elements, without need for firmly linked together. / Fante prepares for a lowMatte elements for such a second pass, with an illuminated white screen behind the setup to Hyams' single key light ships joined together. paint onto white paper. contrast stock, original used as optical printing further duping. 52 • CINEFEX 20 with a streak. Approaching us, the bolt was top-lit; going away, it was bottom-lit.\" The energy burst is actually the Discovery's sole surviving crew member leaving the Stargate. After flying out of the monolith, Dave Bowman returns in spirit to Earth - first to say a final goodbye to his wife and then to visit his dying mother. In her hospital room, only she can see him as he causes her hairbrush to rise up off of a bedside table and brush her hair. In actuality, the hairbrush was filmed separately and matted into the shot, with additional light kicks and shadows created by the animation department. After his mother dies, Bowman next appears on board the Discovery to warn Floyd that he and the rest of the joint mission must leave Jupiter immediately. Walking slowly from the bridge to the pod bay, Bowman first appears as the young man he was when he left the ship some nine years earlier. In successive scenes, he becomes older and older, all the while telling Floyd that something wonderful is about to happen. In his final form, Bowman appears as the Starchild, the enigmatic creature that haunted the final frames of the original 2001. Though the different stages of Bowman's aging were provided by makeup supervisor Michael Westmore, the Starchild itself was designed and built by the EEG model shop where a human-looking baby had to be created to look and move realistically. \"The Starchild was a difficult project,\" Edlund reflected. \"Here was a situation where we had to model our child after the one in the original picture, because we had to have continuity, but Peter also wanted it to be more alive and appear more natural. As a result, we had to do something which I don't think had ever really been done before - to actually animate an artificial human being and make it look absolutely real. The fetus had to look absolutely perfect, without any flaws. It had to look alive, and it had to look right at the camera and be convincing. And it was a situation where a great deal of time and effort was needed to create something that would ultimately be on the screen for only three or four seconds. After trying to do just this one effect, I think I would refute anybody who tried to In 2001, the Starchild had no articulation requirements and could therefore be constructed out of rigid fiberglass. Since Peter Hyams wanted the 2010 baby to be more alive, however, Richard Edlund enlisted Stuart Ziff - then deeply immersed in producing creatures for Ghostbusters - to oversee the project. \"I kept putting it off,\" said Ziff. \"We were really up to our ears in Ghostbusters at the time; and frankly, after all the stuff we were doing for that film, we figured that making the Starchild and having its head turn and eyes move and blink would be relatively trivial - which turned out to be a mistake.\" Assuming that the basic techniques being employed in Ghostbusters could be translated to 2010, Ziff delegated the Starchild project to sculptor Mike Hosch, who began by rendering an exquisite reproduction of the 2001 Starchild - seventeen inches tall from the tip of its oversized head to about mid-torso. Since the one shot required of it was to be framed in tight closeup, the lower torso and legs were deemed unnecesHan, he come tene sary. Once completed, the figure was cast in Schram foam by master moldmaker Gunnar Ferdinandsen and fitted over a fiberglass shell. Though eye and eye-blink mechanisms would be cable-operated, the head and body movements would be handpuppeteered by costumer and mechanical assistant Teresa Burkett, who won the assignment on the basis of small hands and thin wrists. With all the necessary cables being routed down through the neck, only she was able to get her hand up and into the head. From the beginning, the Starchild eyes became a major focus of attention. \"Mike Hosch made the eyes for us,\" explained Tom Culnan, one of the few crew members involved in the project from beginning to end. \"The eyes were actually cast polyester spheres into which a concave cavity was carved, and then a hole drilled into the center about a quarter of an inch in diameter. This became the iris of the eye. Mike would then paint a flat black paint down into the drilled out portion in the center of the iris. Next, using a variety of enamels and acrylic paints with a pearlescent material in them, he would actually paint in the muscle flecks of the iris. Once this was done, he would apply fibers of paint in light pink and red lines to represent veining. Then, again using polyester, he would, in successive batches, pour in the clear-casting polyester over the painted iris until he accumulated a bump that would represent the cornial bump. Generally when people construct eyes for projects like this, they just use a full spherical eye rather than try to recreate that cornial bump which creates a curve under the upper eyelid that's a characteristic of the natural human eye. After that was done, Mike would sand it and shape it and give it a natural look, and then coat it again with a clear finish of the polyester resin to give it that liquid, wet look of a human eye. In fact, as you turned the eye towards a side light source, the light would go through the lens and give you a real starry, kind of glistening effect. It was beautiful.\" Eye movement was also an important consideration. \"Human eyes, if you watch them, tend to converge as you bring the focus point closer to them,\" Culnan continued. \"In 2010, the Starchild begins with his eyes lowered and then he turns and looks up at the camera. What we needed, then, was a cable-operated eyefocusing mechanism that would allow perfect tracking and parallel focusing, as well as the ability to adjust the eyes for focus distance. We had to make it so that as his eyes were coming up, he could - smoothly and naturally - adjust his focus for distance from very close to about seven feet away, where Dr. Floyd was standing. Steve Dunham and I developed a fluid, mechanically-operated cable mechanism where we could actually do this and adjust the eye focus as the eyes tracked up and over to the left.\" Despite the advances being made in certain areas, the Starchild project was in trouble. Unfortunately, nobody seemed to realize it. \"It was my fault, \" Ziff admitted. \"I just didn't give it"],["53","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 53"],["54","2010","enough attention. It was unbelievable. Here I was in the same building with all these guys - walking past them every day - but I couldn't deal with it. I was just too overloaded. Finally we were coming up on the date where we had to show it to Hyams and do a test shooting at M-G-M. I think it was on a friday that we were supposed to do that; and on like the Tuesday or Wednesday before, I decided I'd better really look into the project. And suddenly I realized that nothing was working. On Ghostbusters, we could get away with murder on those creatures because they weren't human; but once we had to work with the human form, we found ourselves in an entirely different situation. The level of technology that worked so well for the first film just didn't work at all on the second. Suddenly I realized that there was about six weeks' worth of work to be done on it, and we had to show it in two days! So I warned Richard that this was going to be a disaster - and it was.\" \"We were under the impression that the Starchild was going to be photographed against a black background - like it was in the original film,\" Culnan elaborated. \"We had shot a 16mm test that way and were fairly well pleased with the look we had achieved. But when we got to M-G-M, we found that it was going to be shot in the pod bay, with full interior lights. Another surprise was that the Starchild was not going to be shot with diffusion, which we were counting on to help conceal any minor surface imperfections - especially since foam latex, unless you paint it with a high gloss paint, has a tendency to look very, very flat under camera lights. So we had some aesthetic questions right from the start. Once we got the puppet set up, we started rehearsing it. Including the puppeteer, there were seven operators involved. At the start of the scene, the head was down. Then it was supposed to come up in a profile and turn - first with the eyes looking into the camera and then with the head tracking over and coming into alignment with the eyes. Unfortunately, the required move was beyond the normal radius of movement for the human wrist and forearm, and as a result started putting a strain on Teresa's arm which made it difficult for her to make subtle attitude adjustments in the head. Also, the extreme movements she was attempting inside the neck tended to restrict the focus and blink cables. All this was pointing out a distinct physiological problem that was going to be difficult to overcome.\" Despite a growing conviction that it was back to the drawing board time, the test was completed and viewed the next morning in dailies. \"In the 70mm format, you saw everything,\" Culnan lamented. \"There was no opportunity whatsoever to hide any minor imperfections. The flatness of the rubber cement painted foam latex became doubly apparent under the lighting that was used. It didn't look at all translucent like real skin, and we began to realize that we'd have to give the whole idea of using foam latex a long, hard look. In addition, the seams which we had attempted to conceal were still painfully visible, and the eyes looked too much like dolls' eyes. Plus the movement problems of the puppeteer were readily apparent in a slight 54 • CINEFEX 20"],["55","2010","quaking in the head and body gestures.\" The first Starchild was, in fact, a total washout. Too exhausted even to contemplate a second start on the Starchild, Stuart Ziff relinquished his supervisory post to Mark Stetson who began by replacing most of the first team with fresh recruits. \"We really had to break a lot of new ground,\" Ziff noted. \"For instance, instead of using Schram foam for the skin, Lance Anderson was brought in and he came up with the idea of using gelatin mixed with glycerine and water - pretty much the same material that vitamin E capsules are made of. The advantage of the gelatin is that it's translucent, and when you put the glycerine in, it doesn't lose its moisture. Lance also did a phenomenal job painting it with transparent dyes mixed in water and alcohol. We also had to make a new one-piece mold with just a single slit down the back side away from the camera. Jon Berg was pulled in to puppeteer the creature, and instead of having someone's hand reach through the neck to rock the head, John Alberti and his father came up with a linkage mechanism that hung down a foot or two below the neck - almost like a periscope on an old submarine. Tom Culnan - one of the few people from the original team - was involved in almost everything, and specifically did a lot of work on the hands and arms, which contained armature joints and were operated externally by rods that extended down from the elbows out of camera range. There was also a slight smile mechanism built into the baby's face and a bladder breathing device. Bob Johnston came up with an excellent radio-controlled mechanism for making the eyes move and track and focus. When real eyes track, if you look off to your left, your right eye has to turn a little more than your left eye. No mechanical creatures I had ever seen could do that - their eyes always tracked together. But Bob came up with a way of doing it and adjusting the parallax, and it looked really good.\" Ziff's personal involvement in the second Starchild was limited primarily to perfecting the blink mechanism he had first developed on Return of the Jedi and then used again on Ghostbusters. \"The standard way to make an eyeblink mechanism is to just have a wire hoop that has a small clearance over the eyeball; and as the hoop rotates around pivot points, the eyelid goes up. The thing is, that only works when the pivot points go through the center diameter of the sphere. And real eyes aren't like that. In fact, the pivot point on a real eye is far from the center. If you were to pivot off-center with a wire hoop, you'd find that as you opened the wire, it would either crash into the sphere or lift far off. So the way nature has dealt with that is that when the eyelid is in its closed position, it is actually at its shortest length, and as it opens, it stretches. Another thing about a real eyelid is that it always rides on the surface of the eye, and the eye isn't spherical in that the cornea has a corneal bump. Of course, a wire hoop isn't going to show that. So what we used, essentially, was a rubber band stretched and tied off on little pivot points, with a flat piece of spring metal to pull it up. And as you pull it up, it stretches the length of the rubber. As a result, the lid rides on the surface like a real eyelid, it changes shape like a real eyelid, and it can adjust to whatever shape is underneath it.\" Six weeks after the initial test, the second Starchild was finished. Though Peter Hyams had originally wanted to shoot it in the pod bay set, the delay had precluded that. Instead, the shot was set up as an insert on one of the EEG stages, with a portion of the bay set brought over from M-G-M to serve as a backdrop. \"We shot all day long and into the night,\" Ziff recalled. \"The baby was mounted on a table about four feet off the ground. Underneath the table was Jon Berg, operating the periscope-like head controller to make the head rock up and down and from side to side. The entire torso was on a separate bearing that could rotate, and this function required a second puppeteer. Two others, one on either side of the table, manned the arm control rods which came out of the elbows. Additional operators controlled the lips and finger movements and Duane Clark did an excellent job of puppeteering the eyes.\" To the relief of all concerned, the Starchild functioned flawlessly for its required three-second performance. Once Floyd sees Bowman, he becomes convinced that indeed he and the rest of the expedition should leave at once. Unfortunately, he has one small problem - due to escalating hostilities between the United States and the Soviet Union, he and his American compatriots have been ordered by their own government to remain on the Discovery, while the Russians have been ordered by their respective government to remain on the Leonov. Since Floyd has determined that the only way for them all to leave is to work together, he ignores his orders and returns to the Leonov to convince Russian commander Tanya Kirbuk (Helen Mirren) that the Leonov and the members of the mission can escape from Jupiter's gravitational pull only by docking with the Discovery and using her engines for the initial burn. Once her fuel is consumed, he reasons, the Discovery can be jettisoned and the Leonov's fuel reserves used for the remainder of the trip home. Since the discussion takes place on the Leonov's zero-gravity bridge, to illustrate his point, Floyd picks up a pen, places it in mid air where it floats, and then picks up another, putting it right above the first one. Then, to show how the Discovery could be discarded after her fuel is expended, Floyd taps the bottom pen with his finger, sending it sailing slowly off across the cabin. Initially, the live-action crew tried to film the floating pens by attaching them to large plates of glass on the set - an approach which had been employed on 2001 - but the pens kept falling off. As a result, Scheider did the scene without any pens at all and just pantomimed the motion of picking them up, leaving Garry Waller to match his movements later during postproduction. After we knew we could do the bluescreen with the tiny spacemen,\" said Waller, \"we felt comfortable with the idea of approaching the floating pens in the same way. Even so, there were still some problems. For instance, in the Leonov cockpit Although the larger-scale miniatures were used for most of the docking scenes, full-length views required that the twelve-and-a-halffoot Discovery be linked with the similarly-scaled six-foot Leonov. Bob Spurlock and Rick Johnson ease the Discovery into place under the already-positioned Leonov. Multiple mounting points on each miniature enabled the supports to be placed in whatever configuration best accommodated the camera. / Once the two ships have been brought together for the shot, Spurlock checks to insure that proper spacing has been maintained. CINEFEX 20 • 55"],["56","2010","The docking maneuver draws to a successful conclusion - and not a moment too soon, for on the surface of alarming that the collective mass is sinking inexorably to the center of the planet, and in the process creating a black hole effect. Unlike the footage, which was flat-lit for economy and flexibility. the black hole compositions Cray computer. / Once the firing the Discovery rockets which signals the end for both the sentient computer and the ship entrusted to Jupiter the monolith is replicating at a rate so earlier-employed Jupiter were lit for maximum impact on Digital Productions' high-power link-up has been made, Chandra coaxes HAL into - an act of self-sacrifice its care. 56 - CINEFEX 20 there were light sources that we had to put onto the pens so they would match, but that was kind of fun. The roughest part was when he first picks the pen up. We had to roto that to match the movement so it would not look too mechanical. We didn't want to attract too much attention. Those were lengthy shots, too, not only in terms of time on the screen, but also in terms of all the roto work involved.\" As he had with the tiny spacemen, Waller filmed the pens on the gimbal device attached to the Oxberry animation stand. Once the Russians agree to the plan, the two ships are maneuvered into position and connected. Unlike most of the shots in the film, where the Leonov and the Discovery were photographed as separate stage elements, the close proximity and contact during the docking sequence required that both ships be photographed together. \"When you're docking two spaceships together without matting them as separate elements, it can be pretty challenging,\" said Dave Stewart. \"That's a continuous shot. It starts on the nose of the Leonov and you follow it with the camera as it moves up and then thrusts to its side and drops down to reveal the Discovery. It's really the first time that you see the relative sizes of the two ships. The Leonou seems really massive because of the rotating crew quarters and things like that. And even in the scenes where the Leonov pulls up in space and parks and you see the Discovery in the distance rotating, the way George and Peter composed the shots, the Leonov is in the foreground so it still looks larger. After all, it is three hundred feet long. But in actual fact, the Discovery is about two and a third times longer. That's why we needed the blowup section of the Discovery to go with the twelve-and-ahalf-foot Leonov. The blowup section had the ball and just the first two or three cargo pods. If it had been the complete model, it would have been twenty-eight feet long.\" After the docking maneuver is completed, HAL activates the Discovery's engines at the proper moment, sending both ships rocketing away from Jupiter. The engine exhausts, like the hand thrusters seen earlier in the film, were created using top-lit animation so they would appear as realistic and as interesting as possible. Many of the engine shots throughout the film were quite lengthy and, as a result, would have required a tremendous number of frames of animation. To avoid this, a cycle was created for the animation so that it could be used over and over again in any one particular shot. \"Any time the ships were cruising, we'd create a cycle,\" Windell explained. \"Then, if they fired up or turned off, we'd do something custom for that by expanding the animation. With an 800 frame shot, we could create a cycle in anywhere from a dozen to sixty frames. Then we'd usually animate a burst or two in there to break up the cycle. So it goes along and then changes for a few frames and then goes along and then changes again for a few frames just to break up the monotony. Most of the hand thruster shots were short, though - only eighteen frames long - so we animated every frame on those without creating a cycle.\" Despite the subtleties of the cyclic animation involved in the engine exhausts, Hyams requested a slightly different look. Although he wasn't certain of exactly what look he wanted, he did know that he wanted it to have a little something more. The solution to this dilemma was eventually found by optical supervisor Mark Vargo who worked closely with the animation department throughout the production. \"Mark created a new kind of ripple effect,\" Windell explained. \"So the exhaust effect ultimately involved three elements - the original stage engine pass, which was of actual lights on the model; our enhancement with animation; and then, when Mark printed the two elements together, he added this little diffusion.\" The diffused rippling effect seemed almost too simple. \"I got a piece of glass and some clear fingernail polish and just threw it on the optical printer, \" Vargo explained. \"I literally just dragged this piece of glass with the polish on it across the face of the gate on the fly to create a sense of energy. It was just something that I decided to try, and I was amazed that it worked. Another thing I worked on for that shot was to soften the edge of the ship, as if there were heat waves around the tail end. I didn't want the ship to look hard, and then have all this activity near the engines.\" As the spacecraft move away from Jupiter, the Discovery fuel is exhausted and the ship is discarded, leaving the Leonov to rush away on its own. Suddenly, Jupiter implodes and then erupts into its new stellar form, a cosmic miracle guided by the unseen aliens who masterminded the monolithic sentinels. Immediately, incredible shock waves burst outward from the new sun, destroying the Discovery and violently rocking the Leonov. \"The Jupiter explosion effect was created out of elements shot by Gary Platek mainly,\" said Richard Edlund. \"It was a laser effect - actually a laser burning through some kind of strange material that he found. In editorial, Conrad Buff went through all this footage and assembled the actual explosion, piece by piece and frame by frame. Then Mark Vargo put it all together and made it work optically. So it was actually a team effort. The shock wave that follows was done by Garry Waller.\" \"The shot where the shock waves overtake the Discovery as the Leonov is trying to get away required five passes - five different colors,\" Waller elaborated. \"What we were trying to do was match a camera flare that someone had done earlier in the show but couldn't get to go past camera. The rings by themselves looked good, but they were just backlit circles of color with black in the center and then black on the outside. So we added some moving density into the center by airbrushing a couple of cels. In fact, we were actually photographing airbrush grain - that's how amazingly sharp 65mm is. At the same time, we had to move in on it as the ring moves toward the camera. The rings were colored red, orange, blue and green, and the superimposed airbrush pattern was white. A real lens flare doesn't have all those colors, but that was the effect we were shooting for. We wanted the rings to appear almost transparent. There is also a sun flare in the center of the expanding rings that"],["57","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 57"],["58","2010","58 • CINEFEX 20"],["59","2010","kind of grows. Conrad Buff put the shot together and masterminded the blends of airbrushed centers and the speed of the shock ring. He also worked in fade-ins and fade-outs. So the effect was really generated in editorial.\" 2010 reaches its thematic climax when the new sun appears in the skies above earth, bringing with it the realization that mankind now has another world to share its solar system with. As the Leonov heads home, the film cuts to a montage of many of the world's most prominent cities - from Cairo to Moscow to London - and over each are two blazing suns. All but one of these shots were actually filmed on location by cameraman Bill Neil and a crew of three who traveled around the world to photograph the necessary background plates. In these shots, Garry Waller added the new sun by creating a pin-hole flare on the Oxberry. This technique produced the sharding effect of the earth sun and the Jovian one, causing their rays to appear to be streaming outward. These were bottom-lit and created a flair that could be laid over the background plates Neil and his crew had shot on location. Only one city could not actually be visited by the EEG/BFC film crew and, as a result, had to be represented in matte painting form by Matthew Yuricich and his assistants. \"We did the shot of Moscow that's at the end of the film,\" Yuricich recalled. \"Personally, I think there were several of the other city shots that we could have done, but Peter wanted the real thing and I can't blame him. He actually sent a crew around the world and they got some good footage of places like the pyramids. Bill Neil realized that they were such a static thing that there was no definition. So to compensate, he hired camel drivers and had them line up on the crest, and it looked great. You are seeing the pyramids, but you're ignoring them. You are watching the camel drivers come down. That you couldn't have done with a painting unless you shot the action and painted in the pyramids.\" Yuricich's Moscow painting was considerably less complicated in execution. For one thing, he didn't have to hire camel drivers. All he had to do, in fact, was find an adequate source for reference and, ironically, when he requested such reference footage, the studio gave him a shot of Moscow from The Shoes of the Fisherman - a scene that was actually a matte painting he himself had done for the 1968 film. \"The Moscow shot was really the only true matte painting in 2010,\" commented matte cameraman Neil Krepela. \"We had, I think, forty-seven paintings in the film, but Moscow really turned out to be the only true matte painting in the traditional sense. It started with the most common view of Moscow - of the Kremlin and St. Basil's Cathedral - and we added big industrial complexes right behind it which belch up these huge amounts of smoke. To get a good smoke plate that we could rear-project into the painting, we went up on top of our stage and Thaine Morris rigged up this smoke trick for us using some smoke bombs that he had. We also came up with some chimneys which we backlit with a whole array of 10Ks. The suns were put in practically. After the foreground Kremlin building elements had been shot and also the background sky painting - which is a hazy area where the two suns were - we took out the sky painting and put up a piece of matte glass with holes cut into it in certain dimensions. Then we shot the two suns separately, each with different filters. Those were burned in and then that came down and we brought in the RP and the two smoke passes. So there were about six passes on that, but it was fun. Essentially, we composited the whole thing on Compsy.\" While the Moscow shot may have been a traditional matte painting assignment for Krepela, the majority of his work on 2010 proved to be anything but typical. In fact, his most complex job was shooting the film's final shot on the Jovian moon of Europa, many years in the future. Moving across the evolving landscape, the camera comes to rest on the nowfamiliar black monolith, a signal that the aliens who have been engineering human destiny since the dawn of man will no doubt be doing the same for the Europans. The origin of the shot - actually a large tabletop miniature - began with Clarke's novel, but was conceived for the film by Peter Hyams. Working with George Jenson, Hyams decided that this final image would be the last of five shots showing Europa's evolution, all of which had to first be brought to life in storyboard form. \"With the Europa change sequence,\" said Jenson, \"we had to deal with colors and try to create a believable landscape. We wanted to keep the colors subtle and not be obvious because we were trying to avoid the cliches you see in every other space movie. So in dealing with an ice planet changing into a green planet, I tried to avoid the obvious green. I used blue-violets and muted earth tones and did everything that I could to avoid seeing what you'd expect to see right away - which would be green trees and leaves and this wonderful summer day. The mood is magic hour, lavender skies and soft trees - all falling into a pattern and yet dealing with very little of the color green. But it says the same thing. It says it with shape, and I think it's kind of fun to avoid the obvious and still make the same point. Also, through that sequence we have a constant composition. The first time we see the change, there are two prominent foreground ice features, and then we go through a storm sequence and the prominent ice features begin to change. Then as we begin our dissolves, we go into the beginning of primitive vegetation, which is falling into more or less the same place in the frame where the ice features were. The sun and the new Jupiter sun are constant in the composition, and through the various changes, the composition is more or less constant until we start to truck in the final shot. Then we truck down, holding on infinity, with our suns moving behind and peeping through the trees and then finally to the monolith. There again, it's the idea of pure, simple design. The vegetation that we see is very nondescript, and we are only inferring and suggesting. \"At one point, we were even kicking around the idea of adding some basic lifeforms. It was one of those ideas where we wouldn't have known whether it was right or wrong until we actually saw it and tested reactions. But I understand why it isn't As the ships accelerate away from Jupiter, Chandra must hoist himself from the Discovery back to the Leonov. Closeups of the perilous passage featured blue flux footage of Bob Balaban composited with appropriate background material, while long shots employed a miniature spaceman animated against blue on the animation stand. / As the Jovian giant begins to collapse in upon itself, the Discovery's fuel is expended and the Leonov separates from it, firing her own rockets in the process. 59 CINEFEX 20 •"],["60","2010","Effects art director George Jenson prepares a watercolor scenes which close the film. Working closely with Peter Hyams and Richard Edlund, Jenson played a major role in determining the overall look of the space shots, as well as the compositional integrity of the effects. / To bridge set was constructed supervisor Henry Millar and primarily of laser elements assembled by editor Conrad animation stand using topand bottom-lit airbrush art. rendering for one of the Europa transformation create the effect of rapid acceleration, the Leonov on a gimbal-mounted platform engineered and built by special effects his crew. / The Jupiter explosion consisted shot by Gary Platek and Buff, followed by a multicolored shock wave effect produced on the 60 • CINEFEX 20 there - because it says too much. If we had lifeforms such as primates, it would have implied that Europa would be like our world, which is more than we wanted to say. But it would have been a great shot. At the beginning of that shot, we talked about having softly-lit primates of some type walking in the water, and as one of them pauses and looks across screen, just for a second, we would then leave him and see what he is looking at - which would be the monolith at the end. It would have tied it all up. But I understand why Peter didn't want to do that.\" Jenson ultimately created a set of watercolor paintings that showed the composition of the shots. Once completed, these were turned over to Mark Stetson in the modelshop to be recreated in tabletop miniature form. In doing so, Stetson had to adhere to the paintings and maintain their composition, because that factor played an important part in the designed impact of the final sequence. \"The different shots are dissolved together but keep the same formal composition and the same sun positions from shot to shot as the planet seems to be changing,\" Stetson elaborated. \"The shots dissolve through this series of almost still images until the last shot, which opens with the same arrangement of the horizon and the suns and foreground elements, and then starts trucking along. Initially, it was tossed back and forth whether this shot was going to be done on a location, as a miniature on a location like a foreground miniature shot, or as a total miniature in the studio. Ultimately, we decided on the latter approach.\" Once that decision was made, Stetson and his crew began constructing the miniature, but they had to do so in a way that would allow the sky background to track with the camera filming the scene. \"The problem for us then became making the sky work with the tabletop and having the sun in apparent parallax with the camera point of view so that it's traveling along behind the forest features. And what we did was to build the tabletop as big as we could fit on the stage, quickly put in a fake backing behind it made out of foam core which we mounted on a motion control track and pulled it through the With the basic setup established for the camera and backing, the next step became building the actual model, which ultimately was about forty feet wide by twenty feet deep. \"The biggest problem was the water. We wanted to do it originally in mylar so we could get some nice scale to it and, by crinkling it up here and there, to roughen up the horizon a little bit. We even worked up a little wave gag by pulling rods under the surface of the mylar to make the water appear to move forward. But that, combined with the crinkling in the foreground, made it sort of snap, crackle and pop, and it wouldn't move smoothly. So rather than trying to work that gag out further, a decision was made to use real water. To get wave effects in the water, we laid down nets on it to break up the background. Also, as the sun moved into position behind the monolith, we wanted an avenue of light down from the horizon so that it would light up the edge of the monolith real nicely. If there was a completely still pool of water, we would have just gotten a spot reflection of the sun. In order to give us this avenue, we threw some netting into the water - a very fine bridal-veil type netting - and that just sat on top. We made it float in some spots and sink down in others, and basically what it did was break the water up a bit. That was a bit tricky, sometimes, because it wanted to settle down during the shot, but we got it to work out nicely.\" To create the vegetation, Jenson found some primitive pine trees in South America that he thought would present a good primordial form of life, and Stetson and his crew used these for reference. \"For the upper parts of the trees, we drilled into dowels, strung bent wire through them and then coated that with spray glue and ground foam. We then heaped up some hemp for body and bought a bunch of plastic aquarium plants, chopped them all up and tufted them into the hemp for the detail trees. A lot of the background trees were just plain hemp. The plant life in the foreground was different, so we made trunks of trees similar to those in the background. Then we got some palm trees from Greg Jein and used the tops of those - the fronds from the tops of the palm trees - as ground cover. We also bought a couple of Madagascar palms and some cactus and put them in the front. The problem was that the plants didn't seem to care too much for the heavy smoke environment we were using during the shot and after several days of shooting, they started to lose their leaves. So we pulled them out of the set, put them outside and fortunately, didn't have to shoot for a few more days. By the time we started shooting again, they were fairly well recovered. \"As the camera tracks down, you come to the monolith, which is standing in the water, and you see its reflection coming out from the bottom of it. Originally, we had the monolith position slightly cheated to correct for the distortion of the lens so it would be nice and upright, but we found that the reflection wasn't working that way so we had to square the monolith to the water pool so the reflection would be nice and straight. We then tilted the camera a little bit to straighten it out in the shot and chopped off the top of the monolith at about a two or three degree angle. It was weird when you looked at it, except through the lens. The top of the monolith had to have a funny angle to it because that was the only way we could make it look right to the camera, since the lens they were using had a lot of pin cushion distortion.\" \"To shoot the miniature, we used cyc lights for the backing,\" Neil Krepela added. \"The suns were simulated with holes cut right through the backing, and the light was shined through 'top frost' diffusion material - it's a diffusion plastic. We used a 1K and a mini-mole as the two respective suns. The diffusion material gave the whole circle of light, but it was thin enough to let a lot of light through on almost a spectral line to the camera, which gave it a lot more punch than straight diffusion and we didn't have to use a 5K back there. On either side of the set, there was a lot of light used as purely bounce. We put up reflectors on the ceiling and I bounced a blue-violet color off of"],["61","2010","CINEFEX 20 • 61"],["62","2010","62 - CINEFEX 20"],["63","2010","parachute material. If you look at a sunset or a morning shot, there is always a fairly warm and bright light as you look into the sun. But as you look up at the sky, it becomes very blue. So any time you measure the color temperature of a scene, you get a warm light off the sun and a pretty bright blue light off the sky - and it is the mix between skylight and sunlight that makes a sunset or a sunrise have the quality that it does. We tried to simulate that with bounce, and since we were smoking up the set to an extreme level, I think the light measurement went down about four stops. So it was pea-soup level, and the bounce cards didn't give quite enough fill, so I had to go in with a couple of hard lights and put a little extra punch on some of the trees in the middle and foregrounds which helped bring out the quality that we wanted - the blue, silhouette look. To soften up everything a little bit more - actually to take out some of the contrast - we put an LC-2 on the camera. It doesn't really soften detail, but it slightly milks out the blacks. The only real strong black we wanted was the monolith, and that was flagged off to keep the punch light off of it and to keep it as black as possible. So the lighting on that miniature, basically, consisted of a lot of soft silhouettes with varying degrees of aerial perspective, and then the monolith.\" Originally, the final sequence was going to be just matte paintings, but the decision to go to models was one that Krepela felt helped give the sequence credibility. \"Using miniatures along with matte paintings adds a little more depth and realism. You can also do little lighting tricks which are very, very difficult to paint in and get away with, especially when there is a natural light in the scene. Most of the suns that we put in we put in practically, and you can get a whole interactive thing going with the film and the lens and the whole business which adds a life to that sort of shot that is almost impossible to reproduce in a painting because it gets too stiff. What also happens is, whenever you paint something, everybody says: 'Ah, that's a painting. That's not quite right. You've got to change that.' You can copy the reference exactly and make it indistinguishable between a photograph and the painting, but it is always in the person's mind that it's a painting, Whenever that occurs, it can be improved on or it's not quite right. Everybody is an expert immediately. 'That's not right. That thing's leaning.' You can show them the reference and explain that the actual object does lean, and it won't make any difference. When you set up a camera in a field and shoot the thing and you get all those same things happening, everyone says, 'Isn't nature wonderful, that you can get this and get that?' You don't think, well, gee, that sure looks funny. But if it's a painting, you've always got to change it.\" Overall, creating the effects for 2010 was a monumental task involving every department at EEG/BFC. Despite the fact that the film was carefully storyboarded, the number of shots grew during production - a common characteristic of any special effects film - causing many departments to find themselves in a crunch for time. The animation department was no exception. \"The biggest freak-out for us,\" Terry Windell recalled, \"was that, as the film went along, we acquired so much more work than we had anticipated. We ended up doing three times as many shots as we had expected, and we acquired probably fifty percent of those extra shots after we began production. Everything was set and deadlined and we went into it and, all of a sudden, the shots were escalating. That was hard to deal with. \"We had dozens of problem shots. We had to deal with some incredible stuff like one shot in the interior of the Leonov. There were two figures in front of a window, and outside there was a sliver of Jupiter and lo and dialog going on - it's a shot where the camera should really have been locked off. But instead, the camera was doing this incredibly smooth dolly, which was really frustrating. We not only had to deal with the figures, but also the changes in the window scale and perspective. Bluescreen would have made it easier for us, but for some of those shots, there were technical reasons, like they wanted a lot of subtle reflections with blue and red light reflecting in the window glass. By using a roto matte, they could show the stars and planets back there, and yet still have the reflections showing on Mark Vargo and his optical department crew experienced an equivalent race against time, compounded by the fact that they must always wait until the very last so that all the other departments can provide them with the various elements needed to complete the shots. Among the particular challenges Vargo faced while working on 2010 was the need to get accustomed to the blue flux matting approach used throughout the film. \"I had some problems with the blue flux material that they shot the front projection bluescreen with. If the projector and camera were located very close to an astronaut, for instance, there seemed to be a blue spill, but only blue spill on the shadow area. So when we ran a piece of negative through our process, or any separation process, the blue tended to flatten out the blacks. The blue from the projector was actually being recorded on film when it would hit the dark side of the suit, and it certainly didn't help that they were in silver suits. That refracted much more than would, say, a buff black material or something like that. So we had to generate various types of contrast mattes that didn't interfere with the highlights, but that would beef up the shadow sides. That took quite a bit of testing, but we were pretty happy with the results.\" Another problem Vargo encountered with the blue flux had to do with a phenomenon know as veiling. \"The guys had basically white suits on and for the way they were being lit on the stage, some of the white somehow wrapped around the back of the suit to create a white fringe, which is only noticeable on the blue flux stuff. So during the filming, they had to remember to put black cloth along the back of their suits so that the light wouldn't reflect off of them. It was a white reflection that was interfering with the transmission of the blue to the screen. It was very odd, so we had to generate cover mattes that were much heavier than As the Leonov heads home, the focus shifts back to earth with a montage of scenic sights, each illuminated by two suns in the sky. Cameraman Bill Neil and a crew of three traveled around the world to photograph the necessary background plates - with the Jovian sun inserted later on the animation stand - but one key city could not be visited. The image of Moscow, therefore, was rendered in matte painting form by Matthew Yuricich, enhanced with smoke and solar effects, and photographed on the Compsy matte stand by Neil Krepela. / Krepela also photographed a progression of tabletop miniatures tracing Europa's evolution from a frozen wasteland to a habitable planet. / Veteran matte painter Matthew Yuricich at work. CINEFEX 20 63"],["64","2010","The final shot in the film miniature. While Ron Gress Dennis Schultz adds foliage to the middleground trees. conifers, bent wires were fed through drilled dowels and then covered with ground aquarium plants. / Water, which was essential to the concept, proved to be the most taxing problem. After an unsuccessful attempt to decision was made to use the real thing. To enhance scale, Leslie Ekker breaks up floating pieces of bridal veil required the largest and most elaborate tabletop airbrushes the scenic backdrop, modelmaker To produce the primitive foam and bits of plastic simulate it with mylar, a the sense of realism and the glassy surface by netting on the water. 64 CINEFEX 20 normal to collapse the white fringe - which looks really bad against stars - and yet stay away from the associated pickup by overexposing the cover matte. And of course, when you collapse a cover matte too far, you have real matte problems because that means the separations are defined inside the edge of an already collapsed black center. But there are some ways around that. Since we had the luxury of a real hard key and a very dark shadow, we could bias the matte and hide the matte line on the shadow side, providing the blacks matched the starfield.\" Despite such problems, Vargo had no qualms about using the front-projected bluescreen process as opposed to any other. \"I don't know how the cameramen felt shooting the plates, or whether they liked one over another, but provided the intensity of the screen is good and the evenness of the illumination is high, I don't care what they shoot it against. We had some great success with the process, and we also had some clumsy, peculiar problems that seem indigenous to that technique. But providing there is no veiling on the back of the guys - which there was quite a bit of - and no blue spill, if it makes it easier to shoot, then it opens up greater possibilities for the principal photography. That is, the ability to take an object great distances and not be constrained to the fact that you've got a forty-foot While he remained somewhat at peace with the blue flux process, Vargo was less enthusiastic about the frontlight/ backlight traveling matte system used on much of the miniature work. \"Frontlight/backlight is plainly an archaic technology, and I think one of the reasons we had to go with the technique was that there was no intermediate model built of the Discovery. They had an eighteen-inch version that had no detail that they used for some spinning shots, but we could never get a medium closeup of that to use as a distant object in space. Instead, they used the big twelve-foot model for all sorts of acrobatic motion in the movie, and, consequently, with that much size, it was difficult for many of the shots to get a rig that could not only move the model, but could also provide room for the blue screen. Also, there were some lighting considerations. We needed a lot of depth of field and everyone was under the impression that we needed a certain amount of fill-to-key to pull a matte. I've done some tests on that and have found that you can light any object however you want against a blue screen using these techniques and you don't need any fill. I can add it and get that harsh terminator back into it all in opticals.\" One of the major difficulties Vargo encountered with the frontlight/backlight approach was getting a good repeat matte pass for shots of the huge Discovery spinning. \"I never was able to composite a shot where the matte repeated perfectly, as it would with a traveling matte system. It just didn't, and that may have been due to the fact that there was all this wattage on the beauty pass all day which may have caused the model to warm up and bend. Then, it was such a complicated process just to get the production pass that they didn't shoot the matte pass until the next day when the model had cooled off. Also, they were working with older equipment, and when you're swinging something that weighs four hundred pounds on an arm, it's too much to expect that it is going to repeat. You've got the size; you've got the depth of field. You know that it's a real disaster when you've got something that is out of focus against a bluescreen. So there are really things to be said for both approaches. But what I thought would have been the simplest solution was to build a four- or five-foot Discovery. Then you could have whipped it around until the cows came home and used bluescreen. In fact, with that you could even get away with doing it frontlight/backlight, because there wouldn't have been the warpage, the setup time would have been a lot less, and it would have been a tighter unit. It was a real struggle to develop a matting technique where we didn't have to take several generations to improve the quality of the mattes, but we managed to do that.\" Some of Vargo's problems on 2010 stemmed from an overzealous first unit that often wanted to create difficult shots. \"There were a few shots in the movie where the first unit violated some absolutes in effects photography,\" Vargo explained. \"For instance, they did a pull-back from a blue screen where they had just little depth of field. The shot had Heywood Floyd and the Russian captain looking out the window at Jupiter, and I think the story point was that the monolith had suddenly disappeared. For dramatic emphasis, they moved closer together and their two heads met at close range - and the whole bluescreen was just a wash. There was no edge left. Whenever you do that, you not only have a real problem resolving the edge when it is out of focus, but also Jupiter and everything else in the background has to rack out of focus at the same speed. So we got that worked out. There was a little bit of chewing where the black-and-white stock wasn't able to resolve - in other words, the efficiency of the blue was totally destroyed and the black-and-white mattes were either switching on or switching off. You get this kind of ravelly looking edge. Then Peter did some tilt-ups on windows - not even against a bluescreen, just against black. So we had to try to extract mattes from the plates where we were going to have to animate the edge. The shot was further compounded by the fact that it was a jerky pan. If it had been smooth, then they could have applied some sort of linear move to it and we could have roughed it in somehow. But, not only was it a jerky pan, but they also had a wide angle lens on there so that as they panned, the struts of the structure became oblique, and we had to roto that feature as well.\" The biggest problem Vargo faced on 2010 was the same he has faced on other films, including Ghostbusters - the reality of time. During the heat of the battle to beat the clock, he and his crew were doing 2.21 takes per approved final optical a day, and needed to do three or four finals each day to finish 2010 in time. As a result, the last dash proved to be quite hair-raising, especially since although the number of shots doubled as the project evolved, Vargo's staff and equipment did not. \"That was"],["65","2010","why it was so important to keep our final ratio as low as possible. And there was that normal amount of redesigning and dropping and adding shots after we spent a lot of time getting them to finals, which drove me crazy. The shots were real long too, and that was another thing that bothered me. Not only did we have twice as many shots, but the length of them was twice to three times as long as on a normal effects movie. So when we had to reshoot something that was maybe a hundred-frame shot and took an hour to shoot, then that meant it became a twohour shot. It was real involved. If we had to reshoot due to a matte problem or an editorial problem, then that long black center had to be remade. So it was a cumulative thing. 2010 was a big show - probably about 350 or 360 shots -and when you end up counting the footage shot, the line-up time and the element time, that's a lot.\" \"In any of the optical shots,\" Edlund added, \"the balance was so critical that we had to use color correction filters that were so pale you could hold them up to the light and barely detect any color in them at all. Mark did an incredible job of balancing them and of optically massaging every aspect of them so that their seams wouldn't show. At the end, he had an incredible record for number of takes needed to get a final version, something like about 2.3 takes to final. That has to be a record; and on many shots, he got them in the first take.\" Vargo, however, was unwilling to accept full credit. \"I had a terrific crew. My camera operators, Chuck Cowles and Bruno George, were absolutely first-rate - as were my line-up people, Ron Moore, Mary Walter, Phil Barberio and Brad Kuehn. Pat Repola processed all our black-and-white mattes, and my optical coordinator, Laurel Walter, made sure everything came together. It was a great group of people, and we really hit our stride on this project.\" Besides animation and optical, the one other department most often hit at the end of a film is editorial. For 2010, visual effects editor Conrad Buff and his staff encountered several unique challenges - problems that often sent them on odysseys of their own through the thousands of feet of footage that accumulated during the production. \"This film, theoretically, was supposed to look realistic,\" Buff said, \"and selecting elements that were not motion control elements was more elusive because of the large volume of material. The shots were also longer and had to sustain themselves on screen longer. As a result, it was more difficult for us to find material that would hold up to scrutiny for three or four times the length of a shot in your typical science fiction film. There were no battle sequences with a lot of quick cuts like you would find in Star Wars. And while there is always a tendency to say in dailies that a particular shot looks great, it's only after you go back and start playing with it that you know whether it will work or not. What we do most is add a sense of timing and rhythm and, in doing so, often have to play the devil's advocate. We know the continuity of the story and we have a better sense of whether something is going to work.\" CINEFEX 20 • 65"],["66","2010","66 - CINEFEX 20"],["67","2010","As with any effects editing assignment, continuity on 2010 was an important key to success - continuity not only in terms of making sure two shots of a spaceship were moving at the same speed, but also in terms of lighting and the dynamics of the particular shot. \"More often than not, our problems were with lighting,\" editor Art Repola noted. \"For example, there might be a two-element shot of a guy on a tether going in front of a ship. The guy was shot on the stage at M-G-M eight months ago, keyed from one side, and the model was shot here and keyed from the other. Sometimes you can just flip one over to fit it in, but sometimes you can't. We're supposed to pay attention to that and weed that out before it gets to optical. Otherwise, optical takes two or three weeks to put it together and it comes up on the screen - and it suddenly has two key lights where there was only supposed to be one. So what we try to do on any film is weed out the bugs so these guys don't have to spend a lot of time putting together something that is obviously wrong. \"On 2010, there were effects where we had to go through frame by frame and build shots from three thousand feet of film some guy spent a week shooting on the stage. One of our functions in editorial is that once the elements are on film, if something is wrong we try to do every photographic trick in the book to make it right so it doesn't have to be reshot on the stage, because stage time is really valuable. So we have to stand on our heads to come up with all the tricks of turning something upside-down, or double printing or dissolving. For instance, there were shots in this film where Conrad was trying to build this contrail out of laser effects that were shot by Gary Platek. And it was one of those things where there were literally eight pieces of film, each a few frames long, which had to be dissolved together to make one short cut. And it turned out that Peter Hyams didn't like it, and so we had to try another technique. \"Another example is a shot in the sequence where they first board the Discovery. They come out of the Leonov and they have jettisoned themselves across to the Discovery and it's still spinning - they haven't gotten inside yet. They get on the ball and the ball is spinning and in the background the stars are going by - in fact, the plate was static but the background makes it look like it's spinning. The stars are going by with Jupiter, and at the end lo comes up and it all matches in sync. Well, the element came through on lo, which was the bottom planet in the shot, and it was shot so that it was gaining on Jupiter as it came up - the speeds were wrong. Rather than go back and have it reshot, I looked at the sequence and determined that I could bring the planet in later and only show it for a second, instead of the three seconds that it was there. So I put it together that way and it worked fine, because it doesn't play long enough for anybody to notice that it was wrong. That's a simple thing, but that's the sort of thing that we do. And maybe it took an hour or two to work it all out and get the move right, and then have it composited in black-and-white to make sure it worked, instead of the many hours it would have taken to reshoot lo on the stage.\" Oftentimes, creating the right continuity in the effects sequences means splicing together one or two frames and then testing the shot out on the Moviola, or even taking a shot done for one sequence and finding out that if slightly altered, it will also fit another sequence. \"That's the fun part - the creative end of it,\" Buff said. \"Supposedly all the shots are storyboarded and people shoot the elements that fit. But we are continually borrowing and stealing from other shots - things that strike us. We say things like: 'Six months ago, I happened to see a little star. It might just be the right thing for the shot I'm working on now.' That's the one thing that I most like about this particular area - editing either in live-action or effects. We understand the story, see all the elements, try to get all the components to fit together, and watch the structure bloom out of that. Then the music and sound effects are added. You work with the film from the start to finish.\" In the end, all of the elements created by so many different people came together to make 2010 a unique film in its own right. \"We have made quantum leaps in the field of special effects since 2001,\" Edlund concluded. \"Even so, there are still some great shots in that film that have not been surpassed to date. The Dawn of Man sequence, for instance, with the apes in front of the monolith, was all shot on a soundstage and is great stuff. And, of course, the Stargate sequence was, for the audiences of 1968, a real mind-blower. But regardless of this, in 2010 we used the state of the art today to provide audiences in 1984 with visual effects that could not have been done in 1968 because of the technology of that day. Just as 2001 was made for the audiences of its day, 2010 was made for the audiences of today: and we think we created a look that has never been seen before in a space movie. I believe the quality of special effects on the screen is the highest ever achieved in a film.\" \"I think we have, in some respects, altered the look of effects,\" Hyams added. \"I don't know if it is to the degree of the first film, however. To me, 2001 and Star Wars literally ignited everybody. I think Kubrick and Lucas really had more to do with making people aware of the limitless possibilities of film than anybody. And I think people like myself are kind of profiteers from all of the work that they did and the risks that they took, and the kind of technical explosion that followed them. Even so, making 2010 took the state of the art; and I was quite lucky, because I just don't think this film could have been made three or four years ago. Actually, I don't think a lot of what we did could have been done. It simply took the best people in the world and the sum total of all their experiences and talent to do it.\" 2010 photographs copyright © 1984 by MGM/UA Entertainment Company. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Bruce McBroom. EEG/BFC still photography by Virgil Mirano. Digital Productions still photography by Peter Reed Miller. Special thanks to Laura Buff. In order to maintain a proper sense of perspective, the sky background was trackmounted and motion controlled, thereby allowing it to move along in conjunction with the camera, which trucked past the Europan setting and came to rest on the monolith. The suns were simulated with stage lights positioned behind holes cut directly into the backing. / During photography, the setting was enveloped in an extremely dense atmosphere of smoke, thus diffusing the imagery and creating a hazy, early moming effect. / 2010's final image - a silent sentinel on the Jovian planet of Europa. CINEFEX 20 • 67"],["68","Closing pages",""]]});
